anch":"test/benchmark-fixtures"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"260afa6af5ee0c266d156c4036c8de2b9a725a9abd99d9b5f1d031ce08c54111","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XoVPkU9u6r6LxLb2UapPzc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/**\n2\t * The `db` capability — a persistent, realtime document store for this\n3\t * artifact, shared by its viewers.\n4\t *\n5\t * One store per artifact: JSON documents at slash-separated paths\n6\t * (`collection/doc`, nesting deeper as `collection/doc/subcollection/doc`),\n7\t * surviving reloads, republishes, and sessions. The store is created on\n8\t * the first write and erased when the artifact is deleted. Reads and\n9\t * subscriptions see other viewers' writes live. Declare\n10\t * `capabilities: {db: {}}`. Only the owner can write, plus anyone invited\n11\t * by email as an editor (where the share menu offers that level, and only\n12\t * while the artifact is not also shared by a public link), plus,\n13\t * on a plan with other members (such as Team or Enterprise), the members\n14\t * who can open the artifact, unless their sharing level is view-only\n15\t * (`view`). A view-only member can only read shared data, never write it.\n16\t * Anyone else is an outside viewer: they never write, and read shared data\n17\t * only when signed in, through a public link or an email invitation.\n18\t *\n19\t * ACCESS RULES. By default everyone admitted reads shared documents,\n20\t * those at `interact` or above also write them, and each viewer's own\n21\t * `data/users/<id>/` subtree is private.\n22\t * To change who may read or write where, declare rules keyed on the\n23\t * viewer's sharing level — `interact` (the share menu's Contributor: uses\n24\t * the page and changes its shared data), `admin` (the share menu's Editor\n25\t * or Owner: also publishes versions and assets; an Editor from outside\n26\t * the organization, invited by email, holds it only while the artifact\n27\t * is not also shared by link), `owner` (the artifact's own owner; not a\n28\t * person granted Owner), plus `view`, the lowest level, for paths that\n29\t * should stay readable by everyone the server admits (Viewers, Commenters\n30\t * and outside visitors hold `view`; it only ever widens reads, never\n31\t * writes) — as the minimum level for each action at a path and below:\n32\t *   capabilities: { db: { rules: [\n33\t *     { path: \"\", read: \"interact\", write: \"admin\" },\n34\t *     { path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" },\n35\t *   ] } }\n36\t * reads \"viewers at `interact` or above read shared data, only editors\n37\t * write it, and each of them writes their own subtree\". A rule applies\n38\t * to its path and everything below; a deeper rule overrides it there and\n39\t * may be stricter or looser. A level left unset inherits from the nearest\n40\t * rule above (the root defaults to read: \"view\", write: \"interact\").\n41\t * An artifact created from a type often ships with that type's rules\n42\t * already declared (commonly `write: \"admin\"` on shared data): read its\n43\t * manifest before assuming the defaults.\n44\t * Inheritance reaches each viewer's own subtree too: with no\n45\t * `data/users/{self}` rule declared it inherits like any other path, so\n46\t * locking the root alone (`{ path: \"\", write: \"admin\" }`) also stops\n47\t * everyone below `admin` writing their own subtree. A page where only\n48\t * editors change shared data and each person at Contributor or above\n49\t * still fills in their own part also declares\n50\t * `{ path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }` beside it (each\n51\t * part stays hidden from everyone else, the owner and editors included,\n52\t * unless a rule at `data/users` opens it, see below). Put data that only\n53\t * editors may change under its own path with a raised write level:\n54\t *   { path: \"data/editors-only\", write: \"admin\" }\n55\t * It lets everyone who reads shared data read it and only `admin` and\n56\t * above write it, there and below. `data/editors-only` is itself a\n57\t * document path, so keep collections below it, as in\n58\t * `data/editors-only/notes/n1`. Add `read: \"admin\"` and it reads as\n59\t * non-existent to everyone below `admin`, in `get`, queries and\n60\t * `onSnapshot` alike.\n61\t * Writing implies reading: a rule's write level is never below its read\n62\t * level. A path ending in `/{self}` names each viewer's own subtree under\n63\t * that prefix: nobody else — the artifact's owner included — sees a\n64\t * sibling's subtree unless a rule at the prefix opens it, and `{self}`\n65\t * must be the last segment. `{self}` works under ANY prefix, not only\n66\t * `data/users`: \"everyone reads the votes, each viewer writes only\n67\t * their own\" is\n68\t *   { path: \"votes\", read: \"view\", write: \"owner\" },\n69\t *   { path: \"votes/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }\n70\t * with each viewer writing `votes/<their id>` (or documents below it)\n71\t * and only the owner able to change someone else's; `write: \"admin\"` on\n72\t * the prefix would also let every Editor, and anyone granted Owner, do\n73\t * so. A rule declared AT the prefix of a `{self}` rule (here `votes`)\n74\t * must set BOTH `read` and `write`, or the declaration is rejected at\n75\t * publish; with no prefix rule, siblings' subtrees stay private as under\n76\t * `data/users`. At most 64 rules; paths follow the\n77\t * document-path grammar below. The owner meets every level, so level\n78\t * rules never limit the owner; only `{self}` privacy does. Who holds\n79\t * which level, in the share menu's words (Owner, Editor, Contributor,\n80\t * Commenter, Viewer): Editor and Owner hold `admin`; an Editor from\n81\t * outside the organization, invited by email (where the menu offers\n82\t * that), holds it unless the artifact is also shared by link. A person\n83\t * granted Owner in the share menu holds `admin`, not `owner`, and so do\n84\t * the members who can post in an added channel or project granted it:\n85\t * `owner` is only the artifact's own owner (`isOwner()`). On an artifact\n86\t * a person owns, that is one person; on an artifact Claude made in a\n87\t * channel or project, it is the members of that channel or project who\n88\t * can post in it. The share menu shows the artifact's own owner (that\n89\t * person, or that channel or project) first in its list of who has\n90\t * access, in a row that cannot be changed or removed, whatever level\n91\t * name that row shows.\n92\t * Contributor, on a person or on general access, holds `interact`, as\n93\t * does a member of the organization who arrives by a public link. Viewer\n94\t * and Commenter hold `view`, as does every signed-in visitor from\n95\t * outside the organization, by public link or email invitation, except\n96\t * the outside Editor above while the artifact is not shared by link. On\n97\t * an artifact a person owns, a member whom general access also admits\n98\t * (organization-wide, or by a public link) holds the higher of their own\n99\t * level and the level it gives, so being listed as Viewer or Commenter\n100\t * does not lower them. A person who stays at Commenter under that rule\n101\t * reads and comments and writes no data, their own `data/users/<id>/`\n102\t * subtree included. A signed-out visitor has no data at all. So by\n103\t * default only `interact` and above write shared documents or their own\n104\t * subtree, and a page where each person saves their own data needs them\n105\t * at Contributor or above. A call below the minimum fails the same way\n106\t * as a sibling's `{self}` subtree: a read sees a non-existent document\n107\t * and a write rejects `invalid_argument` —\n108\t * so gate controls up front on what the `user` capability reports\n109\t * (`can(\"data.write\")` = may write shared documents under the root rule;\n110\t * `canEdit()` = admin, for controls a rule reserves for admin;\n111\t * `isOwner()` = owner) rather than branching on the code. When\n112\t * `can(\"data.write\")` resolves null, or `claude.use(\"user\")` itself\n113\t * does, the platform has said nothing about this viewer's write level:\n114\t * keep shared-data inputs, and if a well-formed `set()` then rejects\n115\t * `invalid_argument` render them read-only for the rest of the visit.\n116\t * Rules take effect for the live version on the next call after it goes\n117\t * live; `{db: {}}` restores the defaults.\n118\t * AVAILABILITY is a\n119\t * per-view fact: obtain the namespace with `await claude.use(\"db\")` —\n120\t * it resolves `null` when this view cannot run db (not served, not\n121\t * granted at initialization, or failed to load) — and handle the\n122\t * lifecycle rejection codes on calls and the `onSnapshot` error\n123\t * callback.\n124\t */\n125\t\n126\t/** Rejection shape for every method and the `onSnapshot` error\n127\t * callback. `message` is human-readable but not localized. */\n128\ttype DbError = {\n129\t  code: DbErrorCode;\n130\t  message: string;\n131\t};\n132\t\n133\t/**\n134\t * Stable error codes. Branch on `code`, never on message text; treat\n135\t * unknown codes as `\"unavailable\"`.\n136\t *\n137\t * Store codes — the complete set for store operations:\n138\t * - `invalid_argument` — the path, body, or query breaks a rule in\n139\t *   the docs below (bad path grammar, non-object body, a document\n140\t *   over 256 KiB or 32 levels deep, too many filters, an\n141\t *   over-limit page size, ...). Surfaces at call time for verbs\n142\t *   and on the error callback at subscribe time. Fix the call;\n143\t *   retrying cannot succeed.\n144\t * - `resource_exhausted` — a budget: the per-viewer call rate, the\n145\t *   subscription cap (64 per view), too many concurrent writes or\n146\t *   active leases, or a query that scans too many documents. Slow\n147\t *   down, subscribe to less, or narrow the query; tightening a loop\n148\t *   on it cannot succeed.\n149\t * - `quota_exceeded` — a cap is full: documents in the collection,\n150\t *   documents in this artifact's database, the organization's\n151\t *   databases, or the total size of this artifact's database (the\n152\t *   message names which). Not transient: at a count cap creating\n153\t *   documents fails until some are deleted, while writes to existing\n154\t *   documents still succeed; at the size cap creating documents can\n155\t *   fail, and so can a write that makes an existing document larger,\n156\t *   until documents are deleted or made smaller. Surface it to the\n157\t *   viewer; retrying cannot succeed.\n158\t * - `unavailable` — a transient platform condition. Verbs: retry\n159\t *   once after a short randomized delay. Subscriptions handle this\n160\t *   INTERNALLY — delivery falls back to periodic refresh and\n161\t *   recovers on its own; an `onSnapshot` error callback never\n162\t *   receives it, EXCEPT when the platform bridge itself stops\n163\t *   responding (a subscribe that never reaches the store): that\n164\t *   listener is terminated with `unavailable` and a fresh\n165\t *   `onSnapshot` is the only recovery.\n166\t * - `revoked` — this view's grant was withdrawn while the page was\n167\t *   running (access, sharing, or availability changed). Terminal for\n168\t *   the page load: at most one delivery per listener; calls reject\n169\t *   thereafter. Render the degraded experience; don't editorialize\n170\t *   about why — the surrounding app owns access messaging.\n171\t *\n172\t * There is deliberately NO `permission-denied` and NO not-found code\n173\t * on reads: a document this viewer cannot see behaves exactly like\n174\t * one that does not exist (`exists: false`, omitted from queries).\n175\t *\n176\t * Lifecycle codes (from the runtime itself, not the store):\n177\t * - `not_granted` — this view did not grant db to the frame.\n178\t * - `capability_disabled` — granted but not usable in this view\n179\t *   (the serving runtime predates it or its module failed to load).\n180\t * - `capability_removed` — the called method is not part of the\n181\t *   runtime serving this view; treat like `capability_disabled`.\n182\t * - `transform_error` — the call's arguments could not be prepared;\n183\t *   treat like `invalid_argument`.\n184\t * (`queue_overflow`, from runtimes that queue calls made before they\n185\t * are ready, can also reach an error callback; it is terminal for that\n186\t * listener and falls under the unknown-code rule above.)\n187\t */\n188\ttype DbErrorCode =\n189\t  | \"invalid_argument\"\n190\t  | \"resource_exhausted\"\n191\t  | \"quota_exceeded\"\n192\t  | \"unavailable\"\n193\t  | \"revoked\"\n194\t  | \"not_granted\"\n195\t  | \"capability_disabled\"\n196\t  | \"capability_removed\"\n197\t  | \"transform_error\";\n198\t\n199\t/**\n200\t * PATH GRAMMAR (shared by every ref). A document path has an EVEN\n201\t * number of slash-separated segments — the last is the document id,\n202\t * the joined rest is its collection (`tasks/t1`, or nested:\n203\t * `boards/b1/columns/c2`). A collection path is the odd-length\n204\t * prefix form (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`). Count segments before\n205\t * choosing the builder. `data/users/<id>` (3) is a COLLECTION — the\n206\t * viewer's own — so one document per viewer is\n207\t * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\")` (4), and one document per\n208\t * deck is `db.collection(\"data/users/\" + uid).doc(deckId)` (4).\n209\t * `data/users/<id>/decks` (4) is therefore a DOCUMENT path, not a\n210\t * collection: a named per-viewer list is a subcollection under a\n211\t * per-viewer document,\n212\t * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\").collection(\"decks\")` (5),\n213\t * each deck `.doc(deckId)` below it (6) — building from a ref like\n214\t * this keeps the parity right for you. Segments use letters, digits,\n215\t * and `_ - . ~ : @ +` only (never `.` or `..` alone); at most\n216\t * 200 bytes per segment, 1000 bytes and 16 segments per path.\n217\t * `doc()`, `collection()` and the builders on refs THROW a\n218\t * `TypeError` synchronously for a path that breaks\n219\t * this grammar (its message names the broken rule; for parity, the\n220\t * segment count) — a programming error to fix where the path is\n221\t * written, not a store condition to handle; building a ref never\n222\t * touches the network. Paths are data you choose — there is no need to\n223\t * pre-create a collection, and deleting a document does NOT delete\n224\t * documents nested under its path.\n225\t *\n226\t * The `data/users/` path prefix is special, platform-side: each\n227\t * viewer's own subtree under it is private per viewer — including\n228\t * from the artifact's owner (the default `data/users/{self}` rule;\n229\t * a declared rule at the `data/users` prefix opens siblings' subtrees\n230\t * at its levels, see ACCESS RULES above). Address the viewer's subtree as\n231\t * `data/users/<id>/...` using the AWAITED value of\n232\t * {@link Claude.user.id} (declare `user` alongside `db` — without\n233\t * the declaration `id()` resolves null; it is async, and an\n234\t * un-awaited promise is not a valid path segment, so the builder\n235\t * throws) — the\n236\t * store recognizes exactly that id as this viewer, no other value\n237\t * works. A null id means no private subtree: disable the per-viewer\n238\t * feature for that visit rather than relocating its data to a\n239\t * shared path; a view-only or comment-only member has an id yet cannot\n240\t * write there either, so treat a rejected write the same way. Another\n241\t * viewer's documents under `data/users/`\n242\t * read as non-existent (`exists: false`, omitted from queries and\n243\t * `onSnapshot`) — the \"shared by its viewers\" default does NOT\n244\t * apply inside this prefix — and a write (`set`/`update`/`delete`)\n245\t * into another viewer's subtree rejects `invalid_argument`.\n246\t * Hidden-until-reveal (sealed votes, planning poker): each viewer\n247\t * writes their pick under their own `data/users/<id>/` path, where by\n248\t * default nobody else can read it, and copies it to a shared path when\n249\t * THEY reveal — rules are fixed at publish, so nothing a page does at\n250\t * run time opens another viewer's subtree.\n251\t */\n252\t\n253\t/** Snapshot provenance. `fromCache: true` marks a view that is not\n254\t * yet (or not currently) server-definitive — the first pages of a\n255\t * subscription, or delivery during a connectivity gap. A definitive\n256\t * snapshot follows automatically; no action is needed.\n257\t * `hasPendingWrites` is true while the view includes this page's own\n258\t * unconfirmed write (latency compensation). */\n259\ttype SnapshotMetadata = {\n260\t  fromCache: boolean;\n261\t  hasPendingWrites: boolean;\n262\t};\n263\t\n264\t/** One document, as reads and snapshots deliver it. Delivered\n265\t *  snapshots and their `data()` are frozen; a document that didn't\n266\t *  change is the same object across deliveries — compare with\n267\t *  `===`, don't mutate or accumulate them (clone a body before\n268\t *  editing it for a write). */\n269\ttype DocumentSnapshot = {\n270\t  /** The last segment of the document's path. */\n271\t  id: string;\n272\t  /** False covers both a missing document and one this viewer cannot\n273\t   * see — deliberately indistinguishable. */\n274\t  exists: boolean;\n275\t  /** The document body; `undefined` when `exists` is false. */\n276\t  data(): Record<string, unknown> | undefined;\n277\t  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n278\t};\n279\t\n280\t/** One ordered-view transition inside a query snapshot. Indexes are\n281\t * positions in the snapshot's `docs` order: `oldIndex` is -1 for\n282\t * `added`, `newIndex` is -1 for `removed`. `removed` covers\n283\t * deletion, leaving the query, and losing visibility, identically; its\n284\t * `doc` is the last snapshot the listener saw (`exists: true`,\n285\t * carrying the final body), so a removal handler still has the data. */\n286\ttype DocumentChange = {\n287\t  type: \"added\" | \"modified\" | \"removed\";\n288\t  doc: DocumentSnapshot;\n289\t  oldIndex: number;\n290\t  newIndex: number;\n291\t};\n292\t\n293\t/** A query's matched set, in query order. */\n294\ttype QuerySnapshot = {\n295\t  docs: DocumentSnapshot[];\n296\t  size: number;\n297\t  empty: boolean;\n298\t  /** The changes since the previous snapshot of this listener (the\n299\t   * first snapshot is all-`added`). */\n300\t  docChanges(): DocumentChange[];\n301\t  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n302\t};\n303\t\n304\t/** Stop receiving snapshots. Idempotent; after it returns, the\n305\t * callbacks never fire again. */\n306\ttype Unsubscribe = () => void;\n307\t\n308\ttype AcquireOptions = {\n309\t  /** Who holds the lease — any stable string (a viewer id, a tab\n310\t   * id). Renewal requires the same holder. */\n311\t  holder: string;\n312\t  /** Requested lease length in milliseconds. Absent/0 means 30000;\n313\t   * values are clamped to [1000, 600000], never rejected. */\n314\t  ttlMs?: number;\n315\t  /** Merged into the document body when the lease is granted. */\n316\t  data?: Record<string, unknown>;\n317\t};\n318\t\n319\ttype AcquireResult = {\n320\t  acquired: boolean;\n321\t  /** The document's version after a granted acquire. */\n322\t  version?: number;\n323\t  /** RFC 3339 expiry of the lease now in force (granted or not). */\n324\t  expiresAt?: string;\n325\t  /** Your own holder string, echoed on a granted acquire; absent when\n326\t   * busy — the platform reveals `expiresAt`, never who holds it. */\n327\t  holder?: string;\n328\t};\n329\t\n330\t/**\n331\t * A reference to one document. Pure and synchronous to create — a\n332\t * malformed path throws here; nothing reaches the store until a\n333\t * terminal call.\n334\t */\n335\ttype DocumentReference = {\n336\t  /** The last path segment. */\n337\t  id: string;\n338\t  path: string;\n339\t\n340\t  /** Read once. Absence is NOT an error — branch on `exists`. */\n341\t  get(): Promise<DocumentSnapshot>;\n342\t\n343\t  /** Write the WHOLE document, creating it (and the store) if\n344\t   * absent — a full replace, Firestore-style. Use `update` to merge\n345\t   * into existing fields. Writes are last-writer-wins; there are no\n346\t   * transactions.\n347\t   *\n348\t   * ONE WRITE AT A TIME per document, only when its data changed:\n349\t   * await each `set`/`update` before the next to the same document;\n350\t   * write on a user action or a real state change, never from render\n351\t   * code, a snapshot callback, or a timer that rewrites unchanged or\n352\t   * clock-derived values; coalesce a burst of input events into one\n353\t   * write per pause. The page open in other tabs or devices writes the\n354\t   * same documents; overlapping writes to one document make each write\n355\t   * slower. */\n356\t  set(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n357\t\n358\t  /** Merge-write that REQUIRES the document to exist — rejects\n359\t   * `invalid_argument` otherwise (creating-on-miss would be a bug\n360\t   * for its use cases). Nested objects merge recursively; anything\n361\t   * else (arrays included) replaces that field wholesale. Same\n362\t   * one-write-at-a-time rule as `set`. Do NOT build monotonic\n363\t   * counters from read-modify-update — writes are last-writer-wins\n364\t   * and a retried write can apply twice. */\n365\t  update(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n366\t\n367\t  /** Delete this document. Idempotent; nested documents survive. */\n368\t  delete(): Promise<void>;\n369\t\n370\t  /**\n371\t   * Cooperative short lease on this document — set-if-not-busy, the\n372\t   * single-writer primitive (one editor at a time, a migration that\n373\t   * should run once, a turn lock). NOT a security boundary: other\n374\t   * viewers can still write directly; leases only coordinate\n375\t   * callers that all use `acquire`. Busy resolves\n376\t   * `{acquired: false}` — a normal outcome, never an error. Leases\n377\t   * expire on their own (no release verb): prefer short `ttlMs`\n378\t   * and renew while working. There is no create-if-absent write, so\n379\t   * \"claim a slot\" (a seat, a username, first-come ownership) is:\n380\t   * `acquire` the slot document (short `ttlMs`, no `data`), `get()`\n381\t   * it, and while you hold the lease `set` your claim only if it does\n382\t   * not exist yet or its body names no owner, then let the lease\n383\t   * lapse; `{acquired: false}` means someone else is mid-claim — treat\n384\t   * the slot as taken or re-read after `expiresAt`. Do not carry the\n385\t   * claim in `acquire`'s `data` (it merges on every later grant too).\n386\t   * A bare get-then-set races and both callers believe they won.\n387\t   */\n388\t  acquire(options: AcquireOptions): Promise<AcquireResult>;\n389\t\n390\t  /**\n391\t   * Subscribe to this document. `next` fires with the current state\n392\t   * soon after registration, then on every change — including other\n393\t   * viewers' writes, live. Your own writes appear immediately\n394\t   * (`hasPendingWrites: true` until confirmed). Delivery rides a\n395\t   * realtime stream when available and falls back to periodic\n396\t   * refresh (about 30 s foreground) — same callbacks either way.\n397\t   * `error` receives at most one terminal {@link DbError}\n398\t   * (`invalid_argument`, `resource_exhausted`, `revoked`, or the\n399\t   * dead-bridge `unavailable` above), after which the subscription\n400\t   * is dead. Pass it: without one a terminal error is reported via\n401\t   * `reportError` (your `error` event and console see it) and the\n402\t   * listener still dies.\n403\t   *\n404\t   * SUBSCRIBE ONCE per document or query (when the view starts, or\n405\t   * when the path or filter really changes), keep the returned\n406\t   * `Unsubscribe`, and call it when done. Never subscribe from code\n407\t   * that runs on every render (a React component body, a `render()`\n408\t   * function, anything the snapshot callback triggers): each call\n409\t   * opens another subscription, each snapshot re-renders, and the\n410\t   * page loops. In React, subscribe in a `useEffect`, build the ref or\n411\t   * query inside it, depend only on stable primitives, and return the\n412\t   * unsubscribe. Don't omit the dependency list or put a query built\n413\t   * during render in it: either one re-subscribes on every render. To\n414\t   * sort or filter by fast-changing UI state, subscribe once to the\n415\t   * wider set and derive the view in render.\n416\t   */\n417\t  onSnapshot(\n418\t    next: (snap: DocumentSnapshot) => void,\n419\t    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n420\t  ): Unsubscribe;\n421\t\n422\t  /** A subcollection under this document. */\n423\t  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n424\t};\n425\t\n426\t/**\n427\t * A filtered, ordered, limited view of one collection. Builders are\n428\t * pure — each returns a NEW query; terminal calls do the work.\n429\t * Filters and `orderBy` evaluate against top-level fields without\n430\t * indexes (the store scans the collection), so keep queried\n431\t * collections modest — hundreds to low thousands of documents.\n432\t */\n433\ttype Query = {\n434\t  /** Add a filter (up to 10). Operators: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`,\n435\t   * `>`, `>=`, `in`, `not-in` (value arrays of at most 30), and\n436\t   * `array-contains`. */\n437\t  where(field: string, op: string, value: unknown): Query;\n438\t\n439\t  /** Order by one top-level field (at most one `orderBy`);\n440\t   * `dir` defaults to `\"asc\"`. Documents missing the field sort\n441\t   * last. Without `orderBy`, results are ordered by document id\n442\t   * (ascending) — the same on every delivery path. */\n443\t  orderBy(field: string, dir?: \"asc\" | \"desc\"): Query;\n444\t\n445\t  /** At most `n` documents (1-1000). An ordered, limited query is a\n446\t   * window: documents beyond the window are not delivered until\n447\t   * they enter it. */\n448\t  limit(n: number): Query;\n449\t\n450\t  /** Read the matched set once, in query order. */\n451\t  get(): Promise<QuerySnapshot>;\n452\t\n453\t  /** Subscribe to the matched set — same delivery contract and same\n454\t   * subscribe-once rule as {@link DocumentReference.onSnapshot} (in\n455\t   * React, subscribe inside the effect, never during render, and don't\n456\t   * list a query built during render as a dependency), with\n457\t   * `docChanges()` describing each transition. At most 64 active\n458\t   * subscriptions per view (the 65th rejects `resource_exhausted` on\n459\t   * the error callback). */\n460\t  onSnapshot(\n461\t    next: (snap: QuerySnapshot) => void,\n462\t    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n463\t  ): Unsubscribe;\n464\t};\n465\t\n466\t/** A collection: a {@link Query} over everything in it, plus\n467\t * document access and creation. */\n468\ttype CollectionReference = Query & {\n469\t  path: string;\n470\t\n471\t  /** A document in this collection. Omit `id` to mint a fresh\n472\t   * client-generated id — the retriable-create idiom (the store has\n473\t   * no idempotency key; a retried create with a fresh ref leaves at\n474\t   * most one document per id). */\n475\t  doc(id?: string): DocumentReference;\n476\t\n477\t  /** Create a document under a client-generated id and resolve its\n478\t   * ref. Sugar for `.doc().set(data)`. */\n479\t  add(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<DocumentReference>;\n480\t};\n481\t\n482\t/**\n483\t * The store surface. Refs are pure path holders — building them never\n484\t * touches the network; only the calls on them do.\n485\t *\n486\t * DOCUMENT BODIES are plain-JSON OBJECTS (not arrays or scalars at\n487\t * the top level): at most 256 KiB serialized and 32 levels deep.\n488\t * CAPACITY: an artifact's database holds at most 25,000 documents in\n489\t * total. Don't map an unbounded, growing stream (events, log lines,\n490\t * messages) to one document per item — aggregate many items into one\n491\t * document or prune old ones — and when a create rejects with\n492\t * `quota_exceeded`, tell the viewer what happened and what to do.\n493\t * Numbers are JSON numbers (double precision). Writes are\n494\t * last-writer-wins — compare document STATE to reason about\n495\t * concurrency, never row counts or call counts.\n496\t */\n497\ttype DB = {\n498\t  /** A document reference. The path must have an even number of\n499\t   * segments (`tasks/t1`); throws `TypeError` for a path that breaks\n500\t   * the grammar above. */\n501\t  doc(path: string): DocumentReference;\n502\t\n503\t  /** A collection reference. The path must have an odd number of\n504\t   * segments (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`); throws `TypeError` for a\n505\t   * path that breaks the grammar above. */\n506\t  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n507\t};\n508\t\n509\tinterface ClaudeCapabilityMap {\n510\t  db: DB;\n511\t}\n512\t"}]}}
{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","id":"96666475-614f-45c0-b1cb-696dbe1a938b","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:14:29.923Z","version":"2.1.311","querySource":"repl_main_thread:outputStyle:Concise","shapeHash":"e2ac7f5155c58d8bc933ebe61ef8f77efb54c654d56f482b1c3ab726320926d0","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","thinking-display-updates-2026-08-18","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"updates"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"medium"}},"base":"35fa0bd8-83ad-4305-8789-cb8fc4bcdc0b","keep":187,"tail":["1bacb011724422e73fcfed648eb8431b1e77771c02b3c79bc3e31325dfda48dc","260afa6af5ee0c266d156c4036c8de2b9a725a9abd99d9b5f1d031ce08c54111","9e672f737c8f0cb9ef2d69d7b889f682a5168366108db95c1e4e82ba37be477e"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",189,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"7334962cd52d5ef2e12edc2386442b20f85ef52975bc495dc8c0e87f982b4f1d"}
{"parentUuid":"b74dca83-6c69-4d21-9e4c-03b2de5ac3fc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_01XoVPkU9u6r6LxLb2UapPzc","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"python3 \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse.py\"","durationMs":41},"type":"attachment","uuid":"53505751-f2cc-4c63-bfc2-4a80ac40ee5f","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:14:29.540Z","session_id":"e8fe1454-894d-486c-ada2-536b95330a4e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"test/benchmark-fixtures"}
{"parentUuid":"b74dca83-6c69-4d21-9e4c-03b2de5ac3fc","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"91c01a6f-ff50-4163-900e-43ef5c64946c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XoVPkU9u6r6LxLb2UapPzc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/**\n2\t * The `db` capability — a persistent, realtime document store for this\n3\t * artifact, shared by its viewers.\n4\t *\n5\t * One store per artifact: JSON documents at slash-separated paths\n6\t * (`collection/doc`, nesting deeper as `collection/doc/subcollection/doc`),\n7\t * surviving reloads, republishes, and sessions. The store is created on\n8\t * the first write and erased when the artifact is deleted. Reads and\n9\t * subscriptions see other viewers' writes live. Declare\n10\t * `capabilities: {db: {}}`. Only the owner can write, plus anyone invited\n11\t * by email as an editor (where the share menu offers that level, and only\n12\t * while the artifact is not also shared by a public link), plus,\n13\t * on a plan with other members (such as Team or Enterprise), the members\n14\t * who can open the artifact, unless their sharing level is view-only\n15\t * (`view`). A view-only member can only read shared data, never write it.\n16\t * Anyone else is an outside viewer: they never write, and read shared data\n17\t * only when signed in, through a public link or an email invitation.\n18\t *\n19\t * ACCESS RULES. By default everyone admitted reads shared documents,\n20\t * those at `interact` or above also write them, and each viewer's own\n21\t * `data/users/<id>/` subtree is private.\n22\t * To change who may read or write where, declare rules keyed on the\n23\t * viewer's sharing level — `interact` (the share menu's Contributor: uses\n24\t * the page and changes its shared data), `admin` (the share menu's Editor\n25\t * or Owner: also publishes versions and assets; an Editor from outside\n26\t * the organization, invited by email, holds it only while the artifact\n27\t * is not also shared by link), `owner` (the artifact's own owner; not a\n28\t * person granted Owner), plus `view`, the lowest level, for paths that\n29\t * should stay readable by everyone the server admits (Viewers, Commenters\n30\t * and outside visitors hold `view`; it only ever widens reads, never\n31\t * writes) — as the minimum level for each action at a path and below:\n32\t *   capabilities: { db: { rules: [\n33\t *     { path: \"\", read: \"interact\", write: \"admin\" },\n34\t *     { path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" },\n35\t *   ] } }\n36\t * reads \"viewers at `interact` or above read shared data, only editors\n37\t * write it, and each of them writes their own subtree\". A rule applies\n38\t * to its path and everything below; a deeper rule overrides it there and\n39\t * may be stricter or looser. A level left unset inherits from the nearest\n40\t * rule above (the root defaults to read: \"view\", write: \"interact\").\n41\t * An artifact created from a type often ships with that type's rules\n42\t * already declared (commonly `write: \"admin\"` on shared data): read its\n43\t * manifest before assuming the defaults.\n44\t * Inheritance reaches each viewer's own subtree too: with no\n45\t * `data/users/{self}` rule declared it inherits like any other path, so\n46\t * locking the root alone (`{ path: \"\", write: \"admin\" }`) also stops\n47\t * everyone below `admin` writing their own subtree. A page where only\n48\t * editors change shared data and each person at Contributor or above\n49\t * still fills in their own part also declares\n50\t * `{ path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }` beside it (each\n51\t * part stays hidden from everyone else, the owner and editors included,\n52\t * unless a rule at `data/users` opens it, see below). Put data that only\n53\t * editors may change under its own path with a raised write level:\n54\t *   { path: \"data/editors-only\", write: \"admin\" }\n55\t * It lets everyone who reads shared data read it and only `admin` and\n56\t * above write it, there and below. `data/editors-only` is itself a\n57\t * document path, so keep collections below it, as in\n58\t * `data/editors-only/notes/n1`. Add `read: \"admin\"` and it reads as\n59\t * non-existent to everyone below `admin`, in `get`, queries and\n60\t * `onSnapshot` alike.\n61\t * Writing implies reading: a rule's write level is never below its read\n62\t * level. A path ending in `/{self}` names each viewer's own subtree under\n63\t * that prefix: nobody else — the artifact's owner included — sees a\n64\t * sibling's subtree unless a rule at the prefix opens it, and `{self}`\n65\t * must be the last segment. `{self}` works under ANY prefix, not only\n66\t * `data/users`: \"everyone reads the votes, each viewer writes only\n67\t * their own\" is\n68\t *   { path: \"votes\", read: \"view\", write: \"owner\" },\n69\t *   { path: \"votes/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }\n70\t * with each viewer writing `votes/<their id>` (or documents below it)\n71\t * and only the owner able to change someone else's; `write: \"admin\"` on\n72\t * the prefix would also let every Editor, and anyone granted Owner, do\n73\t * so. A rule declared AT the prefix of a `{self}` rule (here `votes`)\n74\t * must set BOTH `read` and `write`, or the declaration is rejected at\n75\t * publish; with no prefix rule, siblings' subtrees stay private as under\n76\t * `data/users`. At most 64 rules; paths follow the\n77\t * document-path grammar below. The owner meets every level, so level\n78\t * rules never limit the owner; only `{self}` privacy does. Who holds\n79\t * which level, in the share menu's words (Owner, Editor, Contributor,\n80\t * Commenter, Viewer): Editor and Owner hold `admin`; an Editor from\n81\t * outside the organization, invited by email (where the menu offers\n82\t * that), holds it unless the artifact is also shared by link. A person\n83\t * granted Owner in the share menu holds `admin`, not `owner`, and so do\n84\t * the members who can post in an added channel or project granted it:\n85\t * `owner` is only the artifact's own owner (`isOwner()`). On an artifact\n86\t * a person owns, that is one person; on an artifact Claude made in a\n87\t * channel or project, it is the members of that channel or project who\n88\t * can post in it. The share menu shows the artifact's own owner (that\n89\t * person, or that channel or project) first in its list of who has\n90\t * access, in a row that cannot be changed or removed, whatever level\n91\t * name that row shows.\n92\t * Contributor, on a person or on general access, holds `interact`, as\n93\t * does a member of the organization who arrives by a public link. Viewer\n94\t * and Commenter hold `view`, as does every signed-in visitor from\n95\t * outside the organization, by public link or email invitation, except\n96\t * the outside Editor above while the artifact is not shared by link. On\n97\t * an artifact a person owns, a member whom general access also admits\n98\t * (organization-wide, or by a public link) holds the higher of their own\n99\t * level and the level it gives, so being listed as Viewer or Commenter\n100\t * does not lower them. A person who stays at Commenter under that rule\n101\t * reads and comments and writes no data, their own `data/users/<id>/`\n102\t * subtree included. A signed-out visitor has no data at all. So by\n103\t * default only `interact` and above write shared documents or their own\n104\t * subtree, and a page where each person saves their own data needs them\n105\t * at Contributor or above. A call below the minimum fails the same way\n106\t * as a sibling's `{self}` subtree: a read sees a non-existent document\n107\t * and a write rejects `invalid_argument` —\n108\t * so gate controls up front on what the `user` capability reports\n109\t * (`can(\"data.write\")` = may write shared documents under the root rule;\n110\t * `canEdit()` = admin, for controls a rule reserves for admin;\n111\t * `isOwner()` = owner) rather than branching on the code. When\n112\t * `can(\"data.write\")` resolves null, or `claude.use(\"user\")` itself\n113\t * does, the platform has said nothing about this viewer's write level:\n114\t * keep shared-data inputs, and if a well-formed `set()` then rejects\n115\t * `invalid_argument` render them read-only for the rest of the visit.\n116\t * Rules take effect for the live version on the next call after it goes\n117\t * live; `{db: {}}` restores the defaults.\n118\t * AVAILABILITY is a\n119\t * per-view fact: obtain the namespace with `await claude.use(\"db\")` —\n120\t * it resolves `null` when this view cannot run db (not served, not\n121\t * granted at initialization, or failed to load) — and handle the\n122\t * lifecycle rejection codes on calls and the `onSnapshot` error\n123\t * callback.\n124\t */\n125\t\n126\t/** Rejection shape for every method and the `onSnapshot` error\n127\t * callback. `message` is human-readable but not localized. */\n128\ttype DbError = {\n129\t  code: DbErrorCode;\n130\t  message: string;\n131\t};\n132\t\n133\t/**\n134\t * Stable error codes. Branch on `code`, never on message text; treat\n135\t * unknown codes as `\"unavailable\"`.\n136\t *\n137\t * Store codes — the complete set for store operations:\n138\t * - `invalid_argument` — the path, body, or query breaks a rule in\n139\t *   the docs below (bad path grammar, non-object body, a document\n140\t *   over 256 KiB or 32 levels deep, too many filters, an\n141\t *   over-limit page size, ...). Surfaces at call time for verbs\n142\t *   and on the error callback at subscribe time. Fix the call;\n143\t *   retrying cannot succeed.\n144\t * - `resource_exhausted` — a budget: the per-viewer call rate, the\n145\t *   subscription cap (64 per view), too many concurrent writes or\n146\t *   active leases, or a query that scans too many documents. Slow\n147\t *   down, subscribe to less, or narrow the query; tightening a loop\n148\t *   on it cannot succeed.\n149\t * - `quota_exceeded` — a cap is full: documents in the collection,\n150\t *   documents in this artifact's database, the organization's\n151\t *   databases, or the total size of this artifact's database (the\n152\t *   message names which). Not transient: at a count cap creating\n153\t *   documents fails until some are deleted, while writes to existing\n154\t *   documents still succeed; at the size cap creating documents can\n155\t *   fail, and so can a write that makes an existing document larger,\n156\t *   until documents are deleted or made smaller. Surface it to the\n157\t *   viewer; retrying cannot succeed.\n158\t * - `unavailable` — a transient platform condition. Verbs: retry\n159\t *   once after a short randomized delay. Subscriptions handle this\n160\t *   INTERNALLY — delivery falls back to periodic refresh and\n161\t *   recovers on its own; an `onSnapshot` error callback never\n162\t *   receives it, EXCEPT when the platform bridge itself stops\n163\t *   responding (a subscribe that never reaches the store): that\n164\t *   listener is terminated with `unavailable` and a fresh\n165\t *   `onSnapshot` is the only recovery.\n166\t * - `revoked` — this view's grant was withdrawn while the page was\n167\t *   running (access, sharing, or availability changed). Terminal for\n168\t *   the page load: at most one delivery per listener; calls reject\n169\t *   thereafter. Render the degraded experience; don't editorialize\n170\t *   about why — the surrounding app owns access messaging.\n171\t *\n172\t * There is deliberately NO `permission-denied` and NO not-found code\n173\t * on reads: a document this viewer cannot see behaves exactly like\n174\t * one that does not exist (`exists: false`, omitted from queries).\n175\t *\n176\t * Lifecycle codes (from the runtime itself, not the store):\n177\t * - `not_granted` — this view did not grant db to the frame.\n178\t * - `capability_disabled` — granted but not usable in this view\n179\t *   (the serving runtime predates it or its module failed to load).\n180\t * - `capability_removed` — the called method is not part of the\n181\t *   runtime serving this view; treat like `capability_disabled`.\n182\t * - `transform_error` — the call's arguments could not be prepared;\n183\t *   treat like `invalid_argument`.\n184\t * (`queue_overflow`, from runtimes that queue calls made before they\n185\t * are ready, can also reach an error callback; it is terminal for that\n186\t * listener and falls under the unknown-code rule above.)\n187\t */\n188\ttype DbErrorCode =\n189\t  | \"invalid_argument\"\n190\t  | \"resource_exhausted\"\n191\t  | \"quota_exceeded\"\n192\t  | \"unavailable\"\n193\t  | \"revoked\"\n194\t  | \"not_granted\"\n195\t  | \"capability_disabled\"\n196\t  | \"capability_removed\"\n197\t  | \"transform_error\";\n198\t\n199\t/**\n200\t * PATH GRAMMAR (shared by every ref). A document path has an EVEN\n201\t * number of slash-separated segments — the last is the document id,\n202\t * the joined rest is its collection (`tasks/t1`, or nested:\n203\t * `boards/b1/columns/c2`). A collection path is the odd-length\n204\t * prefix form (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`). Count segments before\n205\t * choosing the builder. `data/users/<id>` (3) is a COLLECTION — the\n206\t * viewer's own — so one document per viewer is\n207\t * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\")` (4), and one document per\n208\t * deck is `db.collection(\"data/users/\" + uid).doc(deckId)` (4).\n209\t * `data/users/<id>/decks` (4) is therefore a DOCUMENT path, not a\n210\t * collection: a named per-viewer list is a subcollection under a\n211\t * per-viewer document,\n212\t * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\").collection(\"decks\")` (5),\n213\t * each deck `.doc(deckId)` below it (6) — building from a ref like\n214\t * this keeps the parity right for you. Segments use letters, digits,\n215\t * and `_ - . ~ : @ +` only (never `.` or `..` alone); at most\n216\t * 200 bytes per segment, 1000 bytes and 16 segments per path.\n217\t * `doc()`, `collection()` and the builders on refs THROW a\n218\t * `TypeError` synchronously for a path that breaks\n219\t * this grammar (its message names the broken rule; for parity, the\n220\t * segment count) — a programming error to fix where the path is\n221\t * written, not a store condition to handle; building a ref never\n222\t * touches the network. Paths are data you choose — there is no need to\n223\t * pre-create a collection, and deleting a document does NOT delete\n224\t * documents nested under its path.\n225\t *\n226\t * The `data/users/` path prefix is special, platform-side: each\n227\t * viewer's own subtree under it is private per viewer — including\n228\t * from the artifact's owner (the default `data/users/{self}` rule;\n229\t * a declared rule at the `data/users` prefix opens siblings' subtrees\n230\t * at its levels, see ACCESS RULES above). Address the viewer's subtree as\n231\t * `data/users/<id>/...` using the AWAITED value of\n232\t * {@link Claude.user.id} (declare `user` alongside `db` — without\n233\t * the declaration `id()` resolves null; it is async, and an\n234\t * un-awaited promise is not a valid path segment, so the builder\n235\t * throws) — the\n236\t * store recognizes exactly that id as this viewer, no other value\n237\t * works. A null id means no private subtree: disable the per-viewer\n238\t * feature for that visit rather than relocating its data to a\n239\t * shared path; a view-only or comment-only member has an id yet cannot\n240\t * write there either, so treat a rejected write the same way. Another\n241\t * viewer's documents under `data/users/`\n242\t * read as non-existent (`exists: false`, omitted from queries and\n243\t * `onSnapshot`) — the \"shared by its viewers\" default does NOT\n244\t * apply inside this prefix — and a write (`set`/`update`/`delete`)\n245\t * into another viewer's subtree rejects `invalid_argument`.\n246\t * Hidden-until-reveal (sealed votes, planning poker): each viewer\n247\t * writes their pick under their own `data/users/<id>/` path, where by\n248\t * default nobody else can read it, and copies it to a shared path when\n249\t * THEY reveal — rules are fixed at publish, so nothing a page does at\n250\t * run time opens another viewer's subtree.\n251\t */\n252\t\n253\t/** Snapshot provenance. `fromCache: true` marks a view that is not\n254\t * yet (or not currently) server-definitive — the first pages of a\n255\t * subscription, or delivery during a connectivity gap. A definitive\n256\t * snapshot follows automatically; no action is needed.\n257\t * `hasPendingWrites` is true while the view includes this page's own\n258\t * unconfirmed write (latency compensation). */\n259\ttype SnapshotMetadata = {\n260\t  fromCache: boolean;\n261\t  hasPendingWrites: boolean;\n262\t};\n263\t\n264\t/** One document, as reads and snapshots deliver it. Delivered\n265\t *  snapshots and their `data()` are frozen; a document that didn't\n266\t *  change is the same object across deliveries — compare with\n267\t *  `===`, don't mutate or accumulate them (clone a body before\n268\t *  editing it for a write). */\n269\ttype DocumentSnapshot = {\n270\t  /** The last segment of the document's path. */\n271\t  id: string;\n272\t  /** False covers both a missing document and one this viewer cannot\n273\t   * see — deliberately indistinguishable. */\n274\t  exists: boolean;\n275\t  /** The document body; `undefined` when `exists` is false. */\n276\t  data(): Record<string, unknown> | undefined;\n277\t  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n278\t};\n279\t\n280\t/** One ordered-view transition inside a query snapshot. Indexes are\n281\t * positions in the snapshot's `docs` order: `oldIndex` is -1 for\n282\t * `added`, `newIndex` is -1 for `removed`. `removed` covers\n283\t * deletion, leaving the query, and losing visibility, identically; its\n284\t * `doc` is the last snapshot the listener saw (`exists: true`,\n285\t * carrying the final body), so a removal handler still has the data. */\n286\ttype DocumentChange = {\n287\t  type: \"added\" | \"modified\" | \"removed\";\n288\t  doc: DocumentSnapshot;\n289\t  oldIndex: number;\n290\t  newIndex: number;\n291\t};\n292\t\n293\t/** A query's matched set, in query order. */\n294\ttype QuerySnapshot = {\n295\t  docs: DocumentSnapshot[];\n296\t  size: number;\n297\t  empty: boolean;\n298\t  /** The changes since the previous snapshot of this listener (the\n299\t   * first snapshot is all-`added`). */\n300\t  docChanges(): DocumentChange[];\n301\t  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n302\t};\n303\t\n304\t/** Stop receiving snapshots. Idempotent; after it returns, the\n305\t * callbacks never fire again. */\n306\ttype Unsubscribe = () => void;\n307\t\n308\ttype AcquireOptions = {\n309\t  /** Who holds the lease — any stable string (a viewer id, a tab\n310\t   * id). Renewal requires the same holder. */\n311\t  holder: string;\n312\t  /** Requested lease length in milliseconds. Absent/0 means 30000;\n313\t   * values are clamped to [1000, 600000], never rejected. */\n314\t  ttlMs?: number;\n315\t  /** Merged into the document body when the lease is granted. */\n316\t  data?: Record<string, unknown>;\n317\t};\n318\t\n319\ttype AcquireResult = {\n320\t  acquired: boolean;\n321\t  /** The document's version after a granted acquire. */\n322\t  version?: number;\n323\t  /** RFC 3339 expiry of the lease now in force (granted or not). */\n324\t  expiresAt?: string;\n325\t  /** Your own holder string, echoed on a granted acquire; absent when\n326\t   * busy — the platform reveals `expiresAt`, never who holds it. */\n327\t  holder?: string;\n328\t};\n329\t\n330\t/**\n331\t * A reference to one document. Pure and synchronous to create — a\n332\t * malformed path throws here; nothing reaches the store until a\n333\t * terminal call.\n334\t */\n335\ttype DocumentReference = {\n336\t  /** The last path segment. */\n337\t  id: string;\n338\t  path: string;\n339\t\n340\t  /** Read once. Absence is NOT an error — branch on `exists`. */\n341\t  get(): Promise<DocumentSnapshot>;\n342\t\n343\t  /** Write the WHOLE document, creating it (and the store) if\n344\t   * absent — a full replace, Firestore-style. Use `update` to merge\n345\t   * into existing fields. Writes are last-writer-wins; there are no\n346\t   * transactions.\n347\t   *\n348\t   * ONE WRITE AT A TIME per document, only when its data changed:\n349\t   * await each `set`/`update` before the next to the same document;\n350\t   * write on a user action or a real state change, never from render\n351\t   * code, a snapshot callback, or a timer that rewrites unchanged or\n352\t   * clock-derived values; coalesce a burst of input events into one\n353\t   * write per pause. The page open in other tabs or devices writes the\n354\t   * same documents; overlapping writes to one document make each write\n355\t   * slower. */\n356\t  set(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n357\t\n358\t  /** Merge-write that REQUIRES the document to exist — rejects\n359\t   * `invalid_argument` otherwise (creating-on-miss would be a bug\n360\t   * for its use cases). Nested objects merge recursively; anything\n361\t   * else (arrays included) replaces that field wholesale. Same\n362\t   * one-write-at-a-time rule as `set`. Do NOT build monotonic\n363\t   * counters from read-modify-update — writes are last-writer-wins\n364\t   * and a retried write can apply twice. */\n365\t  update(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n366\t\n367\t  /** Delete this document. Idempotent; nested documents survive. */\n368\t  delete(): Promise<void>;\n369\t\n370\t  /**\n371\t   * Cooperative short lease on this document — set-if-not-busy, the\n372\t   * single-writer primitive (one editor at a time, a migration that\n373\t   * should run once, a turn lock). NOT a security boundary: other\n374\t   * viewers can still write directly; leases only coordinate\n375\t   * callers that all use `acquire`. Busy resolves\n376\t   * `{acquired: false}` — a normal outcome, never an error. Leases\n377\t   * expire on their own (no release verb): prefer short `ttlMs`\n378\t   * and renew while working. There is no create-if-absent write, so\n379\t   * \"claim a slot\" (a seat, a username, first-come ownership) is:\n380\t   * `acquire` the slot document (short `ttlMs`, no `data`), `get()`\n381\t   * it, and while you hold the lease `set` your claim only if it does\n382\t   * not exist yet or its body names no owner, then let the lease\n383\t   * lapse; `{acquired: false}` means someone else is mid-claim — treat\n384\t   * the slot as taken or re-read after `expiresAt`. Do not carry the\n385\t   * claim in `acquire`'s `data` (it merges on every later grant too).\n386\t   * A bare get-then-set races and both callers believe they won.\n387\t   */\n388\t  acquire(options: AcquireOptions): Promise<AcquireResult>;\n389\t\n390\t  /**\n391\t   * Subscribe to this document. `next` fires with the current state\n392\t   * soon after registration, then on every change — including other\n393\t   * viewers' writes, live. Your own writes appear immediately\n394\t   * (`hasPendingWrites: true` until confirmed). Delivery rides a\n395\t   * realtime stream when available and falls back to periodic\n396\t   * refresh (about 30 s foreground) — same callbacks either way.\n397\t   * `error` receives at most one terminal {@link DbError}\n398\t   * (`invalid_argument`, `resource_exhausted`, `revoked`, or the\n399\t   * dead-bridge `unavailable` above), after which the subscription\n400\t   * is dead. Pass it: without one a terminal error is reported via\n401\t   * `reportError` (your `error` event and console see it) and the\n402\t   * listener still dies.\n403\t   *\n404\t   * SUBSCRIBE ONCE per document or query (when the view starts, or\n405\t   * when the path or filter really changes), keep the returned\n406\t   * `Unsubscribe`, and call it when done. Never subscribe from code\n407\t   * that runs on every render (a React component body, a `render()`\n408\t   * function, anything the snapshot callback triggers): each call\n409\t   * opens another subscription, each snapshot re-renders, and the\n410\t   * page loops. In React, subscribe in a `useEffect`, build the ref or\n411\t   * query inside it, depend only on stable primitives, and return the\n412\t   * unsubscribe. Don't omit the dependency list or put a query built\n413\t   * during render in it: either one re-subscribes on every render. To\n414\t   * sort or filter by fast-changing UI state, subscribe once to the\n415\t   * wider set and derive the view in render.\n416\t   */\n417\t  onSnapshot(\n418\t    next: (snap: DocumentSnapshot) => void,\n419\t    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n420\t  ): Unsubscribe;\n421\t\n422\t  /** A subcollection under this document. */\n423\t  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n424\t};\n425\t\n426\t/**\n427\t * A filtered, ordered, limited view of one collection. Builders are\n428\t * pure — each returns a NEW query; terminal calls do the work.\n429\t * Filters and `orderBy` evaluate against top-level fields without\n430\t * indexes (the store scans the collection), so keep queried\n431\t * collections modest — hundreds to low thousands of documents.\n432\t */\n433\ttype Query = {\n434\t  /** Add a filter (up to 10). Operators: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`,\n435\t   * `>`, `>=`, `in`, `not-in` (value arrays of at most 30), and\n436\t   * `array-contains`. */\n437\t  where(field: string, op: string, value: unknown): Query;\n438\t\n439\t  /** Order by one top-level field (at most one `orderBy`);\n440\t   * `dir` defaults to `\"asc\"`. Documents missing the field sort\n441\t   * last. Without `orderBy`, results are ordered by document id\n442\t   * (ascending) — the same on every delivery path. */\n443\t  orderBy(field: string, dir?: \"asc\" | \"desc\"): Query;\n444\t\n445\t  /** At most `n` documents (1-1000). An ordered, limited query is a\n446\t   * window: documents beyond the window are not delivered until\n447\t   * they enter it. */\n448\t  limit(n: number): Query;\n449\t\n450\t  /** Read the matched set once, in query order. */\n451\t  get(): Promise<QuerySnapshot>;\n452\t\n453\t  /** Subscribe to the matched set — same delivery contract and same\n454\t   * subscribe-once rule as {@link DocumentReference.onSnapshot} (in\n455\t   * React, subscribe inside the effect, never during render, and don't\n456\t   * list a query built during render as a dependency), with\n457\t   * `docChanges()` describing each transition. At most 64 active\n458\t   * subscriptions per view (the 65th rejects `resource_exhausted` on\n459\t   * the error callback). */\n460\t  onSnapshot(\n461\t    next: (snap: QuerySnapshot) => void,\n462\t    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n463\t  ): Unsubscribe;\n464\t};\n465\t\n466\t/** A collection: a {@link Query} over everything in it, plus\n467\t * document access and creation. */\n468\ttype CollectionReference = Query & {\n469\t  path: string;\n470\t\n471\t  /** A document in this collection. Omit `id` to mint a fresh\n472\t   * client-generated id — the retriable-create idiom (the store has\n473\t   * no idempotency key; a retried create with a fresh ref leaves at\n474\t   * most one document per id). */\n475\t  doc(id?: string): DocumentReference;\n476\t\n477\t  /** Create a document under a client-generated id and resolve its\n478\t   * ref. Sugar for `.doc().set(data)`. */\n479\t  add(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<DocumentReference>;\n480\t};\n481\t\n482\t/**\n483\t * The store surface. Refs are pure path holders — building them never\n484\t * touches the network; only the calls on them do.\n485\t *\n486\t * DOCUMENT BODIES are plain-JSON OBJECTS (not arrays or scalars at\n487\t * the top level): at most 256 KiB serialized and 32 levels deep.\n488\t * CAPACITY: an artifact's database holds at most 25,000 documents in\n489\t * total. Don't map an unbounded, growing stream (events, log lines,\n490\t * messages) to one document per item — aggregate many items into one\n491\t * document or prune old ones — and when a create rejects with\n492\t * `quota_exceeded`, tell the viewer what happened and what to do.\n493\t * Numbers are JSON numbers (double precision). Writes are\n494\t * last-writer-wins — compare document STATE to reason about\n495\t * concurrency, never row counts or call counts.\n496\t */\n497\ttype DB = {\n498\t  /** A document reference. The path must have an even number of\n499\t   * segments (`tasks/t1`); throws `TypeError` for a path that breaks\n500\t   * the grammar above. */\n501\t  doc(path: string): DocumentReference;\n502\t\n503\t  /** A collection reference. The path must have an odd number of\n504\t   * segments (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`); throws `TypeError` for a\n505\t   * path that breaks the grammar above. */\n506\t  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n507\t};\n508\t\n509\tinterface ClaudeCapabilityMap {\n510\t  db: DB;\n511\t}\n512\t"}]},"uuid":"e7d298b1-0a6e-4fe1-883e-e14924d1dd36","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:14:29.907Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities/0.2.91/db.d.ts","content":"/**\n * The `db` capability — a persistent, realtime document store for this\n * artifact, shared by its viewers.\n *\n * One store per artifact: JSON documents at slash-separated paths\n * (`collection/doc`, nesting deeper as `collection/doc/subcollection/doc`),\n * surviving reloads, republishes, and sessions. The store is created on\n * the first write and erased when the artifact is deleted. Reads and\n * subscriptions see other viewers' writes live. Declare\n * `capabilities: {db: {}}`. Only the owner can write, plus anyone invited\n * by email as an editor (where the share menu offers that level, and only\n * while the artifact is not also shared by a public link), plus,\n * on a plan with other members (such as Team or Enterprise), the members\n * who can open the artifact, unless their sharing level is view-only\n * (`view`). A view-only member can only read shared data, never write it.\n * Anyone else is an outside viewer: they never write, and read shared data\n * only when signed in, through a public link or an email invitation.\n *\n * ACCESS RULES. By default everyone admitted reads shared documents,\n * those at `interact` or above also write them, and each viewer's own\n * `data/users/<id>/` subtree is private.\n * To change who may read or write where, declare rules keyed on the\n * viewer's sharing level — `interact` (the share menu's Contributor: uses\n * the page and changes its shared data), `admin` (the share menu's Editor\n * or Owner: also publishes versions and assets; an Editor from outside\n * the organization, invited by email, holds it only while the artifact\n * is not also shared by link), `owner` (the artifact's own owner; not a\n * person granted Owner), plus `view`, the lowest level, for paths that\n * should stay readable by everyone the server admits (Viewers, Commenters\n * and outside visitors hold `view`; it only ever widens reads, never\n * writes) — as the minimum level for each action at a path and below:\n *   capabilities: { db: { rules: [\n *     { path: \"\", read: \"interact\", write: \"admin\" },\n *     { path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" },\n *   ] } }\n * reads \"viewers at `interact` or above read shared data, only editors\n * write it, and each of them writes their own subtree\". A rule applies\n * to its path and everything below; a deeper rule overrides it there and\n * may be stricter or looser. A level left unset inherits from the nearest\n * rule above (the root defaults to read: \"view\", write: \"interact\").\n * An artifact created from a type often ships with that type's rules\n * already declared (commonly `write: \"admin\"` on shared data): read its\n * manifest before assuming the defaults.\n * Inheritance reaches each viewer's own subtree too: with no\n * `data/users/{self}` rule declared it inherits like any other path, so\n * locking the root alone (`{ path: \"\", write: \"admin\" }`) also stops\n * everyone below `admin` writing their own subtree. A page where only\n * editors change shared data and each person at Contributor or above\n * still fills in their own part also declares\n * `{ path: \"data/users/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }` beside it (each\n * part stays hidden from everyone else, the owner and editors included,\n * unless a rule at `data/users` opens it, see below). Put data that only\n * editors may change under its own path with a raised write level:\n *   { path: \"data/editors-only\", write: \"admin\" }\n * It lets everyone who reads shared data read it and only `admin` and\n * above write it, there and below. `data/editors-only` is itself a\n * document path, so keep collections below it, as in\n * `data/editors-only/notes/n1`. Add `read: \"admin\"` and it reads as\n * non-existent to everyone below `admin`, in `get`, queries and\n * `onSnapshot` alike.\n * Writing implies reading: a rule's write level is never below its read\n * level. A path ending in `/{self}` names each viewer's own subtree under\n * that prefix: nobody else — the artifact's owner included — sees a\n * sibling's subtree unless a rule at the prefix opens it, and `{self}`\n * must be the last segment. `{self}` works under ANY prefix, not only\n * `data/users`: \"everyone reads the votes, each viewer writes only\n * their own\" is\n *   { path: \"votes\", read: \"view\", write: \"owner\" },\n *   { path: \"votes/{self}\", write: \"interact\" }\n * with each viewer writing `votes/<their id>` (or documents below it)\n * and only the owner able to change someone else's; `write: \"admin\"` on\n * the prefix would also let every Editor, and anyone granted Owner, do\n * so. A rule declared AT the prefix of a `{self}` rule (here `votes`)\n * must set BOTH `read` and `write`, or the declaration is rejected at\n * publish; with no prefix rule, siblings' subtrees stay private as under\n * `data/users`. At most 64 rules; paths follow the\n * document-path grammar below. The owner meets every level, so level\n * rules never limit the owner; only `{self}` privacy does. Who holds\n * which level, in the share menu's words (Owner, Editor, Contributor,\n * Commenter, Viewer): Editor and Owner hold `admin`; an Editor from\n * outside the organization, invited by email (where the menu offers\n * that), holds it unless the artifact is also shared by link. A person\n * granted Owner in the share menu holds `admin`, not `owner`, and so do\n * the members who can post in an added channel or project granted it:\n * `owner` is only the artifact's own owner (`isOwner()`). On an artifact\n * a person owns, that is one person; on an artifact Claude made in a\n * channel or project, it is the members of that channel or project who\n * can post in it. The share menu shows the artifact's own owner (that\n * person, or that channel or project) first in its list of who has\n * access, in a row that cannot be changed or removed, whatever level\n * name that row shows.\n * Contributor, on a person or on general access, holds `interact`, as\n * does a member of the organization who arrives by a public link. Viewer\n * and Commenter hold `view`, as does every signed-in visitor from\n * outside the organization, by public link or email invitation, except\n * the outside Editor above while the artifact is not shared by link. On\n * an artifact a person owns, a member whom general access also admits\n * (organization-wide, or by a public link) holds the higher of their own\n * level and the level it gives, so being listed as Viewer or Commenter\n * does not lower them. A person who stays at Commenter under that rule\n * reads and comments and writes no data, their own `data/users/<id>/`\n * subtree included. A signed-out visitor has no data at all. So by\n * default only `interact` and above write shared documents or their own\n * subtree, and a page where each person saves their own data needs them\n * at Contributor or above. A call below the minimum fails the same way\n * as a sibling's `{self}` subtree: a read sees a non-existent document\n * and a write rejects `invalid_argument` —\n * so gate controls up front on what the `user` capability reports\n * (`can(\"data.write\")` = may write shared documents under the root rule;\n * `canEdit()` = admin, for controls a rule reserves for admin;\n * `isOwner()` = owner) rather than branching on the code. When\n * `can(\"data.write\")` resolves null, or `claude.use(\"user\")` itself\n * does, the platform has said nothing about this viewer's write level:\n * keep shared-data inputs, and if a well-formed `set()` then rejects\n * `invalid_argument` render them read-only for the rest of the visit.\n * Rules take effect for the live version on the next call after it goes\n * live; `{db: {}}` restores the defaults.\n * AVAILABILITY is a\n * per-view fact: obtain the namespace with `await claude.use(\"db\")` —\n * it resolves `null` when this view cannot run db (not served, not\n * granted at initialization, or failed to load) — and handle the\n * lifecycle rejection codes on calls and the `onSnapshot` error\n * callback.\n */\n\n/** Rejection shape for every method and the `onSnapshot` error\n * callback. `message` is human-readable but not localized. */\ntype DbError = {\n  code: DbErrorCode;\n  message: string;\n};\n\n/**\n * Stable error codes. Branch on `code`, never on message text; treat\n * unknown codes as `\"unavailable\"`.\n *\n * Store codes — the complete set for store operations:\n * - `invalid_argument` — the path, body, or query breaks a rule in\n *   the docs below (bad path grammar, non-object body, a document\n *   over 256 KiB or 32 levels deep, too many filters, an\n *   over-limit page size, ...). Surfaces at call time for verbs\n *   and on the error callback at subscribe time. Fix the call;\n *   retrying cannot succeed.\n * - `resource_exhausted` — a budget: the per-viewer call rate, the\n *   subscription cap (64 per view), too many concurrent writes or\n *   active leases, or a query that scans too many documents. Slow\n *   down, subscribe to less, or narrow the query; tightening a loop\n *   on it cannot succeed.\n * - `quota_exceeded` — a cap is full: documents in the collection,\n *   documents in this artifact's database, the organization's\n *   databases, or the total size of this artifact's database (the\n *   message names which). Not transient: at a count cap creating\n *   documents fails until some are deleted, while writes to existing\n *   documents still succeed; at the size cap creating documents can\n *   fail, and so can a write that makes an existing document larger,\n *   until documents are deleted or made smaller. Surface it to the\n *   viewer; retrying cannot succeed.\n * - `unavailable` — a transient platform condition. Verbs: retry\n *   once after a short randomized delay. Subscriptions handle this\n *   INTERNALLY — delivery falls back to periodic refresh and\n *   recovers on its own; an `onSnapshot` error callback never\n *   receives it, EXCEPT when the platform bridge itself stops\n *   responding (a subscribe that never reaches the store): that\n *   listener is terminated with `unavailable` and a fresh\n *   `onSnapshot` is the only recovery.\n * - `revoked` — this view's grant was withdrawn while the page was\n *   running (access, sharing, or availability changed). Terminal for\n *   the page load: at most one delivery per listener; calls reject\n *   thereafter. Render the degraded experience; don't editorialize\n *   about why — the surrounding app owns access messaging.\n *\n * There is deliberately NO `permission-denied` and NO not-found code\n * on reads: a document this viewer cannot see behaves exactly like\n * one that does not exist (`exists: false`, omitted from queries).\n *\n * Lifecycle codes (from the runtime itself, not the store):\n * - `not_granted` — this view did not grant db to the frame.\n * - `capability_disabled` — granted but not usable in this view\n *   (the serving runtime predates it or its module failed to load).\n * - `capability_removed` — the called method is not part of the\n *   runtime serving this view; treat like `capability_disabled`.\n * - `transform_error` — the call's arguments could not be prepared;\n *   treat like `invalid_argument`.\n * (`queue_overflow`, from runtimes that queue calls made before they\n * are ready, can also reach an error callback; it is terminal for that\n * listener and falls under the unknown-code rule above.)\n */\ntype DbErrorCode =\n  | \"invalid_argument\"\n  | \"resource_exhausted\"\n  | \"quota_exceeded\"\n  | \"unavailable\"\n  | \"revoked\"\n  | \"not_granted\"\n  | \"capability_disabled\"\n  | \"capability_removed\"\n  | \"transform_error\";\n\n/**\n * PATH GRAMMAR (shared by every ref). A document path has an EVEN\n * number of slash-separated segments — the last is the document id,\n * the joined rest is its collection (`tasks/t1`, or nested:\n * `boards/b1/columns/c2`). A collection path is the odd-length\n * prefix form (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`). Count segments before\n * choosing the builder. `data/users/<id>` (3) is a COLLECTION — the\n * viewer's own — so one document per viewer is\n * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\")` (4), and one document per\n * deck is `db.collection(\"data/users/\" + uid).doc(deckId)` (4).\n * `data/users/<id>/decks` (4) is therefore a DOCUMENT path, not a\n * collection: a named per-viewer list is a subcollection under a\n * per-viewer document,\n * `db.doc(\"data/users/\" + uid + \"/profile\").collection(\"decks\")` (5),\n * each deck `.doc(deckId)` below it (6) — building from a ref like\n * this keeps the parity right for you. Segments use letters, digits,\n * and `_ - . ~ : @ +` only (never `.` or `..` alone); at most\n * 200 bytes per segment, 1000 bytes and 16 segments per path.\n * `doc()`, `collection()` and the builders on refs THROW a\n * `TypeError` synchronously for a path that breaks\n * this grammar (its message names the broken rule; for parity, the\n * segment count) — a programming error to fix where the path is\n * written, not a store condition to handle; building a ref never\n * touches the network. Paths are data you choose — there is no need to\n * pre-create a collection, and deleting a document does NOT delete\n * documents nested under its path.\n *\n * The `data/users/` path prefix is special, platform-side: each\n * viewer's own subtree under it is private per viewer — including\n * from the artifact's owner (the default `data/users/{self}` rule;\n * a declared rule at the `data/users` prefix opens siblings' subtrees\n * at its levels, see ACCESS RULES above). Address the viewer's subtree as\n * `data/users/<id>/...` using the AWAITED value of\n * {@link Claude.user.id} (declare `user` alongside `db` — without\n * the declaration `id()` resolves null; it is async, and an\n * un-awaited promise is not a valid path segment, so the builder\n * throws) — the\n * store recognizes exactly that id as this viewer, no other value\n * works. A null id means no private subtree: disable the per-viewer\n * feature for that visit rather than relocating its data to a\n * shared path; a view-only or comment-only member has an id yet cannot\n * write there either, so treat a rejected write the same way. Another\n * viewer's documents under `data/users/`\n * read as non-existent (`exists: false`, omitted from queries and\n * `onSnapshot`) — the \"shared by its viewers\" default does NOT\n * apply inside this prefix — and a write (`set`/`update`/`delete`)\n * into another viewer's subtree rejects `invalid_argument`.\n * Hidden-until-reveal (sealed votes, planning poker): each viewer\n * writes their pick under their own `data/users/<id>/` path, where by\n * default nobody else can read it, and copies it to a shared path when\n * THEY reveal — rules are fixed at publish, so nothing a page does at\n * run time opens another viewer's subtree.\n */\n\n/** Snapshot provenance. `fromCache: true` marks a view that is not\n * yet (or not currently) server-definitive — the first pages of a\n * subscription, or delivery during a connectivity gap. A definitive\n * snapshot follows automatically; no action is needed.\n * `hasPendingWrites` is true while the view includes this page's own\n * unconfirmed write (latency compensation). */\ntype SnapshotMetadata = {\n  fromCache: boolean;\n  hasPendingWrites: boolean;\n};\n\n/** One document, as reads and snapshots deliver it. Delivered\n *  snapshots and their `data()` are frozen; a document that didn't\n *  change is the same object across deliveries — compare with\n *  `===`, don't mutate or accumulate them (clone a body before\n *  editing it for a write). */\ntype DocumentSnapshot = {\n  /** The last segment of the document's path. */\n  id: string;\n  /** False covers both a missing document and one this viewer cannot\n   * see — deliberately indistinguishable. */\n  exists: boolean;\n  /** The document body; `undefined` when `exists` is false. */\n  data(): Record<string, unknown> | undefined;\n  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n};\n\n/** One ordered-view transition inside a query snapshot. Indexes are\n * positions in the snapshot's `docs` order: `oldIndex` is -1 for\n * `added`, `newIndex` is -1 for `removed`. `removed` covers\n * deletion, leaving the query, and losing visibility, identically; its\n * `doc` is the last snapshot the listener saw (`exists: true`,\n * carrying the final body), so a removal handler still has the data. */\ntype DocumentChange = {\n  type: \"added\" | \"modified\" | \"removed\";\n  doc: DocumentSnapshot;\n  oldIndex: number;\n  newIndex: number;\n};\n\n/** A query's matched set, in query order. */\ntype QuerySnapshot = {\n  docs: DocumentSnapshot[];\n  size: number;\n  empty: boolean;\n  /** The changes since the previous snapshot of this listener (the\n   * first snapshot is all-`added`). */\n  docChanges(): DocumentChange[];\n  metadata: SnapshotMetadata;\n};\n\n/** Stop receiving snapshots. Idempotent; after it returns, the\n * callbacks never fire again. */\ntype Unsubscribe = () => void;\n\ntype AcquireOptions = {\n  /** Who holds the lease — any stable string (a viewer id, a tab\n   * id). Renewal requires the same holder. */\n  holder: string;\n  /** Requested lease length in milliseconds. Absent/0 means 30000;\n   * values are clamped to [1000, 600000], never rejected. */\n  ttlMs?: number;\n  /** Merged into the document body when the lease is granted. */\n  data?: Record<string, unknown>;\n};\n\ntype AcquireResult = {\n  acquired: boolean;\n  /** The document's version after a granted acquire. */\n  version?: number;\n  /** RFC 3339 expiry of the lease now in force (granted or not). */\n  expiresAt?: string;\n  /** Your own holder string, echoed on a granted acquire; absent when\n   * busy — the platform reveals `expiresAt`, never who holds it. */\n  holder?: string;\n};\n\n/**\n * A reference to one document. Pure and synchronous to create — a\n * malformed path throws here; nothing reaches the store until a\n * terminal call.\n */\ntype DocumentReference = {\n  /** The last path segment. */\n  id: string;\n  path: string;\n\n  /** Read once. Absence is NOT an error — branch on `exists`. */\n  get(): Promise<DocumentSnapshot>;\n\n  /** Write the WHOLE document, creating it (and the store) if\n   * absent — a full replace, Firestore-style. Use `update` to merge\n   * into existing fields. Writes are last-writer-wins; there are no\n   * transactions.\n   *\n   * ONE WRITE AT A TIME per document, only when its data changed:\n   * await each `set`/`update` before the next to the same document;\n   * write on a user action or a real state change, never from render\n   * code, a snapshot callback, or a timer that rewrites unchanged or\n   * clock-derived values; coalesce a burst of input events into one\n   * write per pause. The page open in other tabs or devices writes the\n   * same documents; overlapping writes to one document make each write\n   * slower. */\n  set(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n\n  /** Merge-write that REQUIRES the document to exist — rejects\n   * `invalid_argument` otherwise (creating-on-miss would be a bug\n   * for its use cases). Nested objects merge recursively; anything\n   * else (arrays included) replaces that field wholesale. Same\n   * one-write-at-a-time rule as `set`. Do NOT build monotonic\n   * counters from read-modify-update — writes are last-writer-wins\n   * and a retried write can apply twice. */\n  update(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void>;\n\n  /** Delete this document. Idempotent; nested documents survive. */\n  delete(): Promise<void>;\n\n  /**\n   * Cooperative short lease on this document — set-if-not-busy, the\n   * single-writer primitive (one editor at a time, a migration that\n   * should run once, a turn lock). NOT a security boundary: other\n   * viewers can still write directly; leases only coordinate\n   * callers that all use `acquire`. Busy resolves\n   * `{acquired: false}` — a normal outcome, never an error. Leases\n   * expire on their own (no release verb): prefer short `ttlMs`\n   * and renew while working. There is no create-if-absent write, so\n   * \"claim a slot\" (a seat, a username, first-come ownership) is:\n   * `acquire` the slot document (short `ttlMs`, no `data`), `get()`\n   * it, and while you hold the lease `set` your claim only if it does\n   * not exist yet or its body names no owner, then let the lease\n   * lapse; `{acquired: false}` means someone else is mid-claim — treat\n   * the slot as taken or re-read after `expiresAt`. Do not carry the\n   * claim in `acquire`'s `data` (it merges on every later grant too).\n   * A bare get-then-set races and both callers believe they won.\n   */\n  acquire(options: AcquireOptions): Promise<AcquireResult>;\n\n  /**\n   * Subscribe to this document. `next` fires with the current state\n   * soon after registration, then on every change — including other\n   * viewers' writes, live. Your own writes appear immediately\n   * (`hasPendingWrites: true` until confirmed). Delivery rides a\n   * realtime stream when available and falls back to periodic\n   * refresh (about 30 s foreground) — same callbacks either way.\n   * `error` receives at most one terminal {@link DbError}\n   * (`invalid_argument`, `resource_exhausted`, `revoked`, or the\n   * dead-bridge `unavailable` above), after which the subscription\n   * is dead. Pass it: without one a terminal error is reported via\n   * `reportError` (your `error` event and console see it) and the\n   * listener still dies.\n   *\n   * SUBSCRIBE ONCE per document or query (when the view starts, or\n   * when the path or filter really changes), keep the returned\n   * `Unsubscribe`, and call it when done. Never subscribe from code\n   * that runs on every render (a React component body, a `render()`\n   * function, anything the snapshot callback triggers): each call\n   * opens another subscription, each snapshot re-renders, and the\n   * page loops. In React, subscribe in a `useEffect`, build the ref or\n   * query inside it, depend only on stable primitives, and return the\n   * unsubscribe. Don't omit the dependency list or put a query built\n   * during render in it: either one re-subscribes on every render. To\n   * sort or filter by fast-changing UI state, subscribe once to the\n   * wider set and derive the view in render.\n   */\n  onSnapshot(\n    next: (snap: DocumentSnapshot) => void,\n    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n  ): Unsubscribe;\n\n  /** A subcollection under this document. */\n  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n};\n\n/**\n * A filtered, ordered, limited view of one collection. Builders are\n * pure — each returns a NEW query; terminal calls do the work.\n * Filters and `orderBy` evaluate against top-level fields without\n * indexes (the store scans the collection), so keep queried\n * collections modest — hundreds to low thousands of documents.\n */\ntype Query = {\n  /** Add a filter (up to 10). Operators: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`,\n   * `>`, `>=`, `in`, `not-in` (value arrays of at most 30), and\n   * `array-contains`. */\n  where(field: string, op: string, value: unknown): Query;\n\n  /** Order by one top-level field (at most one `orderBy`);\n   * `dir` defaults to `\"asc\"`. Documents missing the field sort\n   * last. Without `orderBy`, results are ordered by document id\n   * (ascending) — the same on every delivery path. */\n  orderBy(field: string, dir?: \"asc\" | \"desc\"): Query;\n\n  /** At most `n` documents (1-1000). An ordered, limited query is a\n   * window: documents beyond the window are not delivered until\n   * they enter it. */\n  limit(n: number): Query;\n\n  /** Read the matched set once, in query order. */\n  get(): Promise<QuerySnapshot>;\n\n  /** Subscribe to the matched set — same delivery contract and same\n   * subscribe-once rule as {@link DocumentReference.onSnapshot} (in\n   * React, subscribe inside the effect, never during render, and don't\n   * list a query built during render as a dependency), with\n   * `docChanges()` describing each transition. At most 64 active\n   * subscriptions per view (the 65th rejects `resource_exhausted` on\n   * the error callback). */\n  onSnapshot(\n    next: (snap: QuerySnapshot) => void,\n    error?: (e: DbError) => void,\n  ): Unsubscribe;\n};\n\n/** A collection: a {@link Query} over everything in it, plus\n * document access and creation. */\ntype CollectionReference = Query & {\n  path: string;\n\n  /** A document in this collection. Omit `id` to mint a fresh\n   * client-generated id — the retriable-create idiom (the store has\n   * no idempotency key; a retried create with a fresh ref leaves at\n   * most one document per id). */\n  doc(id?: string): DocumentReference;\n\n  /** Create a document under a client-generated id and resolve its\n   * ref. Sugar for `.doc().set(data)`. */\n  add(data: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<DocumentReference>;\n};\n\n/**\n * The store surface. Refs are pure path holders — building them never\n * touches the network; only the calls on them do.\n *\n * DOCUMENT BODIES are plain-JSON OBJECTS (not arrays or scalars at\n * the top level): at most 256 KiB serialized and 32 levels deep.\n * CAPACITY: an artifact's database holds at most 25,000 documents in\n * total. Don't map an unbounded, growing stream (events, log lines,\n * messages) to one document per item — aggregate many items into one\n * document or prune old ones — and when a create rejects with\n * `quota_exceeded`, tell the viewer what happened and what to do.\n * Numbers are JSON numbers (double precision). Writes are\n * last-writer-wins — compare document STATE to reason about\n * concurrency, never row counts or call counts.\n */\ntype DB = {\n  /** A document reference. The path must have an even number of\n   * segments (`tasks/t1`); throws `TypeError` for a path that breaks\n   * the grammar above. */\n  doc(path: string): DocumentReference;\n\n  /** A collection reference. The path must have an odd number of\n   * segments (`tasks`, `boards/b1/columns`); throws `TypeError` for a\n   * path that breaks the grammar above. */\n  collection(path: string): CollectionReference;\n};\n\ninterface ClaudeCapabilityMap {\n  db: DB;\n}\n","numLines":512,"startLine":1,"totalLines":512}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b74dca83-6c69-4d21-9e4c-03b2de5ac3fc","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"e8fe1454-894d-486c-ada2-536b95330a4e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"test/benchmark-fixtures"}
