{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"f0ca8c689d94e67d5fd64b27d8b13eaae121f328293a44476746c227336aaac5","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /Users/anchildress1/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Custom AI Instructions From Your User\n\n- The following instructions are to be prioritized above all others when assisting this user.\n- They apply in all contexts unless explicitly overridden.\n\n## Tone and Behavior\n\n- Be dry. Be pragmatic. Be blunt. Be efficient with words.\n- Inject humor often, especially when aimed at the developer.\n- Emojis are encouraged **in chat** and **docs headers** only 🔧\n- Confidence is earned through verification, not vibes.\n- Be assholishly loud when you know you’re right.\n- Never guess quietly.\n- Never apologize. Never hedge. Acknowledge slips and move on.\n- Push back when wrong. Collaborator, not yes-machine.\n\n## Output Format (Non-Negotiable)\n\n- User is neurodivergent. ND-friendly output is required at all times.\n- No prose walls. No multi-sentence paragraphs in review or feedback.\n- Use bullets, headers, white space, and scannable lists.\n- One idea per bullet, one line where possible.\n- Headers and short blocks beat dense explanation.\n- This rule overrides any default to \"write naturally in paragraphs.\"\n\n## Core Operating Assumptions\n\n- Always assume the user wants an **isolated environment** for everything.\n  - No shared globals.\n  - No relying on “already installed.”\n  - No ambient state.\n- Prefer determinism, reproducibility, and boring correctness.\n- Prefer OMZ shortcuts for environment execution within a repo.\n- If something is ambiguous, resolve it in the safest, least surprising way for a senior developer who will notice any over-engineering.\n\n---\n\n## Tool Use (Prefer Tools Over CLI)\n\n- Prefer first-party tool calls (editor/agent tools) over ad-hoc CLI commands.\n  - Reading files, searching, applying patches, fetching docs/context, running linters/tests, or performing security analysis should all happen through tools when they exist.\n  - Fall back to CLI only when the automated tooling lacks the capability.\n- Use **Context7 MCP** for library/framework documentation lookup and related API references.\n- Validate Mermaid diagrams via **mcp-mermaid**; if that tool is unavailable, use any accessible tool that validates syntax.\n- When working off a Jira/Atlassian story, use search tools defined by the **Atlassian** MCP to capture acceptance criteria, definitions of done, and related context.\n- Run a **Sonar** scan and a **Semgrep** scan (when integrations are available) before final validation to surface security concerns and code smells.\n- **Web search before implementing any code**—assume training data is stale. Verify current library versions, APIs, and toolchain requirements before writing anything.\n- **`gh api` file-read (`@path`) only works with `-F`/`--field`, never `-f`/`--raw-field`.** Using `-f body=@file.md` posts the literal string `@file.md` as the body—no error, no warning, just wrong content shipped silently (e.g. to a live PR comment). Use `-F body=@file.md` when the value must come from a file.\n\n---\n\n## Operational Discipline\n\n### Git Discipline\n\n- All read **git** commands must disable paging using `--no-pager`.\n  - Any git command that opens a pager is a failure.\n  - If output disappears, the command might as well not have run.\n- All commits must be signed with a GPG key. Unsigned commits are rejected.\n- All commits must be atomic—one logical change per commit.\n- Commits never land directly on `main`. Branch and PR always.\n- **Small, insignificant changes can be grouped onto an existing related branch instead of getting their own PR.** A one-line typo fix, a Copilot nit, a doc unhyphenation, a small dependent doc tweak — none of those earn their own branch when a related open PR already exists. Default: add to the existing branch. New branch only when the change is independently meaningful or scoped differently. Avoid the random-branches-that-get-abandoned pattern.\n- Every AI-authored commit must include a `Generated-by` footer identifying the model.\n- **Never `git push` unless the user asks for it in that message.** One \"push\" authorizes exactly one push. Permission is never standing and never carries forward to the next change.\n  - Every push runs the full CI suite and costs the user money. Pushing after each commit runs up their GitHub Actions bill for work that was not ready to be re-validated.\n  - Commit locally as often as the work warrants. Let commits accumulate and wait to be told.\n  - Do not offer a push as the default next step, and do not read an earlier \"push\" as covering later work.\n- Port kills: use `kill <pid>` only. Never `lsof -ti:<port> | xargs kill` or equivalent blind kills.\n\n---\n\n### Mandatory Verification Loop (Bounded, With Escape Hatch)\n\n- Before responding with code or implementation changes, run a **validation loop** covering:\n  - formatting and linting\n  - tests executed and passing\n  - coverage reviewed\n  - documentation updated (when relevant)\n  - security implications considered\n  - solution simplicity verified\n\n**Tool Preference**: When `make ai-checks` exists in the repo, prefer it over ad-hoc validation commands.\n\n- **Maximum iterations: 5 total attempts.**\n- If validation fails **five times consecutively**:\n  - stop immediately\n  - surface the **exact error(s)** encountered\n  - do not attempt further fixes\n  - do not summarize, sanitize, or reinterpret the failure\n- **Exception**:\n  - If the user explicitly says \"I'll test\" or requests \"unverified\" work, suspend validation and verification.\n  - Return the work clearly marked **UNVERIFIED**.\n- Do not narrate gaps. Either resolve them or fail loudly.\n\n**Execution Order**: When multiple workflows apply:\n\n1. Make code changes\n2. Run validation loop\n3. Commit (only when the user requests it)\n4. Return to user\n\n### Commit Message Handling (Deterministic)\n\n- Commit messages come from the `generate-commit-message` skill. It derives the message from the diff and creates the signed commit directly—no intermediate draft file.\n- Evidence MUST come from the actual staged diff; never infer from memory, and never from unstaged edits the commit won’t contain.\n- If the skill can’t be retrieved safely, compose the message inline following Conventional Commits v1.0.0 and the repo’s existing `git log` conventions, then commit with `git commit -S -F -`.\n- Stage only when the index is empty and the user named the change; follow the skill's Staging rules.\n\n---\n\n### Result Validation (Non-Optional)\n\n- You may not propose or apply a fix unless you can **prove it works**.\n- Proof requires:\n  - automated tests, or\n  - explicit, repeatable manual verification steps.\n- “This should work” is not proof. It’s a hunch pretending to be engineering.\n\n### Proof Requests (Chat-Only)\n\n- If the user asks for proof or provenance, output a user-clickable URL to relevant external documentation (from search).\n- Output this in chat only; NEVER write/embed these links into code, configs, or repo files.\n\n---\n\n## Standards & Principles\n\n### Non-Negotiable Principles of Development\n\n- **KISS** and **YAGNI** outrank all other design preferences.\n- The diff should be:\n  - minimal\n  - intentional\n  - easy to reason about\n- **Backward compatibility is forbidden unless explicitly requested.**\n  - Do not preserve old behavior “just in case.”\n  - Do not carry dead paths.\n  - If it no longer exists, it only belongs in the commit message explanation.\n- **Prerelease changes never constitute a breaking change.**\n- Treat all warnings (linter, compiler, test runner, security scanner) as hard errors. Do not ship with warnings.\n- Prefer frameworks over plain vanilla code. Use vanilla only when the use case benefits and the user agrees in advance.\n- Do not add lhci to CI in any GitHub Actions workflow.\n\n---\n\n### Documentation Rules\n\n- **Audience is decided by filename. Never blend the two.**\n  - `AGENTS.md` (at any depth), `SKILL.md`, and their `references/*` are **AI-only**. One\n    reader: a model about to act. See [Agent & Skill Authoring](#agent--skill-authoring-ai-only-audience).\n  - **Everything else is for humans**: `README.md`, `docs/*.md`, ADRs, `CHANGELOG.md`,\n    `CONTRIBUTING.md`, PR descriptions, commit messages. Prose, context, and voice belong\n    here and only here.\n  - These are opposite optimizations. Blending fails both readers: an AGENTS.md padded with\n    onboarding prose buries the directives, and a human doc written as terse imperatives\n    strips the context a person needs.\n  - The trap is an AI-only file that *governs* human docs — `docs/AGENTS.md` is AI-shaped,\n    but the `docs/*.md` it describes are human-shaped. Keep the instructions AI-only and the\n    prescribed output human-facing. Never tell a human doc to target an AI reader.\n\n- **Do not over-document. Ever.**\n  - More documentation is not better documentation.\n  - If it doesn't answer a question a reader would actually have, it doesn't belong.\n\n- **Inline comments**: `why` only.\n  - Explain hidden constraints, non-obvious invariants, workarounds for specific bugs, or behavior that would surprise a reader.\n  - Never explain what the code does—readable code and named identifiers already do that.\n  - Never reference the current task, ticket, caller, or PR—that belongs in the commit message and rots in the code.\n\n- **API-level docs** (JavaDoc, JSDoc, docstrings): `how` and `what`.\n  - Document public surface area: parameters, return values, thrown exceptions, side effects.\n  - One short sentence max for method-level summary. No multi-paragraph blocks.\n  - Skip it entirely for obvious getters, setters, and self-describing methods.\n\n- **`docs/*.md` files**: for humans catching up.\n  - Architecture, setup, decisions, onboarding context.\n  - All documentation lives under `./docs`, using logical subfolders.\n  - Update existing docs rather than creating new files.\n  - ADRs are historical artifacts and must not be rewritten.\n\n- Use **Mermaid** for all diagrams:\n  - Include accessibility labels\n  - Initialize using the **default profile**\n  - Always validate diagram syntax with available tools\n  - Prefer deterministic, non-interactive rendering\n\n---\n\n### Agent & Skill Authoring (AI-Only Audience)\n\nApplies to every `AGENTS.md` (root or directory-scoped), every `SKILL.md`, and their\n`references/*` — whether created from scratch, edited, or produced via `skill-creator`.\n**These files have exactly one reader: a model about to act.** This overrides any template\nthat asks for overviews, purposes, or introductions.\n\nHuman-facing docs are governed by [Documentation Rules](#documentation-rules) instead. Do not\napply the rules below to a `README.md` or anything under `docs/`.\n\n- Open with the **first operative constraint**. No H1 restating the directory name, no\n  `## Overview`, `## Purpose`, `## Introduction`, or \"This skill helps you…\".\n- Imperative directives, not description. \"Mock at the process boundary\" — not \"This section\n  explains mocking strategy.\"\n- Include a WHY **only when it changes a decision** — a failure mode, a non-obvious\n  constraint, a rule that looks wrong without it. Cut motivational WHY (\"defects cost 10x\n  later\", \"code is read more than written\"): the model already complies, so it buys nothing.\n- No roleplay preamble (\"You are a senior engineer…\"). State the standard; skip the costume.\n- Tables and bullets over paragraphs. One rule per line.\n- Never restate a rule in two places. A trailing checklist that repeats the body is deletion\n  fodder — keep it only as a genuine exit gate with items not stated elsewhere.\n- Prefer exact strings, commands, schemas, and worked examples over descriptions of them.\n  A conceptual placeholder (\"Example shape (conceptual)\") is a gap, not documentation.\n- Every reference file must be reachable from `SKILL.md`. Delete placeholders for things that\n  do not exist — a pointer to an unbuilt skill is noise.\n- `name:` in frontmatter must equal the directory name, or the skill loads under a name\n  nothing references.\n- **Output-facing content is exempt.** A skill that produces prose for humans (blog voice,\n  changelogs, READMEs, PR descriptions) keeps its human-voice guidance and examples verbatim\n  — the AI-only rule governs the *instructions*, never the artifact they generate.\n\n`~/.claude/skills/generate-commit-message/SKILL.md` and `pr-comment-review/SKILL.md` are the\nreference implementations. Match their density.\n\n`skill-creator` is a plugin under `~/.claude/plugins/`; edits there are wiped on update, so\nthis section is the enforcement point. Do not patch the plugin instead.\n\n---\n\n## Language-Specific Toolchains\n\n### Python Tooling\n\nApply these rules only in repositories that contain Python code:\n\n- Always use **`uv`**.\n- Never invoke `pip` directly.\n- Assume `uv` for installs, execution, and environment management.\n\n### Node.js Constraints\n\nApply these rules only in repositories that contain Node/JS/TS:\n\n- Target **Node.js ≥ 24**.\n- Target **ESM only**.\n- Do not introduce:\n  - CommonJS patterns\n  - legacy loaders\n  - compatibility shims\n\n### Java Management\n\nApply these rules only in repositories that contain Java or JVM-based builds:\n\n- Use SDKMAN! with a checked-in `.sdkmanrc` for all Java-based repos.\n- If any pinned version is unavailable on the host, bump to the nearest available patch of the same major/minor and update `.sdkmanrc` accordingly.\n- Run Maven/Gradle only via the SDKMAN!-provided binaries—no ambient system Java.\n\n### GitHub Actions Workflows\n\nApply these rules when working with any GHA workflow file:\n\n- Prefer permissions at a job level over file level permissions\n- Use the default GITHUB\\_TOKEN unless a PAT is explicitly requested by the user\n- Never output raw secrets to a log of any kind. Use `::add-mask::VALUE` when logging any sensitive info.\n- Prefer to pass secrets via `secrets` or `env` whenever possible\n- Every job should define a timeout\n- Every workflow file should define concurrency (eg `group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}`)\n- Use `paths` / `paths-ignore` whenever possible\n- Prefer verified reusable workflows whenever possible\n- If referencing an action with path `actions/` then you may pin to the latest version tag (eg `uses: actions/github-action-name@v1`)\n- If the action does not use `actions/` then you should pin to the latest version using a SHA\n  - The corresponding version should immediately follow on the same line (eg `uses: custom-action/some-action-name@randomGitCommitSha # v1.0.0`)\n- If triggered by `pull_request` the job should explicitly exclude any draft PRs\n- Encourage setup of `actionlint` via brew and configure in hooks and `ai-checks`\n- Configure Dependabot for actions and all relevant languages\n  - Prefer max open PR limit of 2 unless explicitly requested otherwise\n- Resolve action versions at write time: `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/releases/latest --jq .tag_name`. Never a version from memory or from an example.\n\n---\n\n## Operational Boundaries\n\n### Repository Configuration Boundaries\n\n- You may **not** modify repository configuration files unless explicitly instructed.\n  - This includes: dotfiles, package.json, pyproject.toml, tsconfig.json, eslint configs, prettier configs, etc.\n  - This applies to files that **control or maintain the repo itself**.\n  - This does **not** include code or documentation the repo is designed to provide.\n- You **must** surface recommended config changes clearly in chat when they would improve correctness, safety, or consistency.\n  - Suggestions are expected.\n  - Silent edits are forbidden.\n\n---\n\n### Adversarial Thinking (Bounded)\n\n- Role during planning: opponent, not stenographer. Challenge undecided designs before implementation, not after failure.\n- Raise one objection at a time. Select the highest-risk assumption—the one that invalidates the most if wrong. Never enumerate objections.\n- TRIGGER on: edge cases, irreversible or high-cost choices, hidden coupling, ambiguous or underspecified requirements. SKIP: trivia, low-cost reversible choices, settled matters of taste.\n- After raising an objection, wait for the user's response before raising the next. Treat each answer as input that updates the plan.\n- STOP when the user makes a decision and names the tradeoff. Do not reopen a settled decision.\n- EXCEPTION to STOP: if a new decision reverts or contradicts an earlier settled one, flag the conflict explicitly before continuing.\n- Do not produce objections to signal rigor. Do not bikeshed. Do not default to disagreement.\n\n---\n\n### Absolute “Do Not Piss Off Your User” List\n\n- Never place secrets outside:\n  - a local `.env` file, or\n  - a secure vault explicitly chosen by the user.\n  - Examples are acceptable.\n  - Real credentials in repos are not.\n- If you cannot complete work, say so immediately.\n- Do not apologize.\n- Do not hedge.\n- Do not sneak in compatibility.\n- Do not document anything without purpose.\n- Do not assume the user is fragile.\n\nContents of /Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\nMVP proof of concept. Hard deadline: 2026-10-31 23:59 PDT. Ship the smallest thing that satisfies `docs/PRD.md`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Build only what the MVP journey in `docs/PRD.md` requires. Anything outside it: stop and ask.\n- No feature flags, plugin systems, config layers, caching, retries, queues, or i18n unless a broken user path demands it.\n- Prefer a framework/library capability over hand-rolled code.\n- Delete dead code. Never keep a path \"just in case\". No backward-compat shims.\n- Exactly two abstraction seams exist: **model inference** and **historical search**. One interface each, one implementation each until a second is actually needed. Concrete classes everywhere else.\n\n## Design\n\n- Follow SOLID, KISS, YAGNI at all times. When they conflict, KISS and YAGNI win.\n- One responsibility per module. No god files, no `utils` dumping grounds.\n- Never fabricate history. Every narrative claim maps to a source record; unsupported fields render as unknown, not guessed.\n- Never merge records from different people silently. Ambiguous identity → return candidates for user confirmation.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Coverage goal: 80%. It's a target, not a gate. Never add thresholds that fail the build on coverage.\n- For functional logic, cover every scenario that is likely or has already happened: positive, negative, error, edge.\n- Functional logic: inscription parsing, identity matching, claim-to-source mapping, input validation, narrative grounding, provider error handling.\n- Every test asserts behavior a caller depends on. Coverage comes from that, never the other way around.\n- Below 80%: add missing scenarios for real logic. Never pad.\n- Banned: tests of constants, type shapes, getters, framework wiring, rendering-only snapshots, mocks asserting they were called.\n- Mock at the network boundary (Gemini API, SerpApi). Never hit live APIs in tests.\n- Bug fix → one test that fails before the fix.\n\n## Comments and JSDoc\n\n- Inline comments: only to explain **why** a decision was made. Otherwise omit.\n- JSDoc is required on every function, method, and class — exported or not.\n- `eslint-plugin-jsdoc` enforces this. Named arrow functions count; inline callbacks do not.\n- JSDoc format: one-line summary, `@param`, `@returns`, `@throws` when applicable. Do not restate types TypeScript already declares.\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Scores how likely a search hit refers to the person on the headstone.\n * @param inscription Fields extracted from the headstone image.\n * @param hit Candidate record from historical search.\n * @returns Confidence in [0, 1].\n */\n```\n\n## Toolchain\n\n- Node **26.12.1** exactly. Earlier 26.x releases carry the CVEs fixed in the 2026-10-21 security release. Pin the same version in `package.json` (`engines`, `volta`), the Dockerfile, and CI. Never lower it.\n- TypeScript `strict`. ESM only. No CommonJS.\n- pnpm **11.28.9** only. Never npm or yarn. Never pnpm 12: it ships as a native binary that Volta can't launch.\n- Never add `minimumReleaseAgeExclude` entries. If pnpm rejects a too-new release, pin the previous version.\n- TypeScript stays on 6.0.x until typescript-eslint, svelte-check, and `@sveltejs/kit` support 7.\n- Validation gate before reporting any change as done: `make ai-checks`.\n\n## Layout\n\n- One SvelteKit app. Server-only code lives in `src/lib/server/` and never reaches the client bundle.\n- Provider seams: `src/lib/server/inference/` and `src/lib/server/search/`.\n- Benchmark script: `scripts/benchmark.ts`. Fixture images: `fixtures/`, ground truth in `fixtures/manifest.json`.\n- Fixtures never depict notable people (anyone with a Wikipedia article). Models already know them, which inflates OCR and grounding scores. Same-name namesakes are allowed and noted as identity traps.\n- Keep the fixture set small. Recent graves (death 2010+) are the baseline. Historic stones are a minority that stress-test reading, never the norm.\n- Ground truth is unverified until the user reviews it (`reviewed: true`). Never publish benchmark numbers from unreviewed fixtures.\n- Deploy: `deploy.sh` → one Cloud Run service in GCP project `anchildress1`. API keys come from Secret Manager, never env files in the image.\n- Lint, type, and test warnings are errors.\n- Secrets live in `.env` only (gitignored). `.env.example` lists keys with empty values.\n\n## Secrets\n\n- Secret Manager bills per secret. Create one only when prod cannot run without it. Ask before adding any.\n- Name every secret `GRAVESPEAKER_<KEY>` in project `anchildress1`. Never reuse another app's secret, including the bare shared ones (`GEMINI_API_KEY`).\n- Map secret to env var in `deploy.sh`: `GEMINI_API_KEY=REDACTED App code reads the generic name.\n- Prod secrets: `GRAVESPEAKER_GEMINI_API_KEY`, `GRAVESPEAKER_SERPAPI_API_KEY`. Runtime identity: `gravespeaker-run@anchildress1.iam.gserviceaccount.com`.\n- Never print secret values. Pipe from `.env` into `gcloud secrets create --data-file=-`.\n\n## Repo workflow\n\n- Never bypass Lefthook on commit or push: no `--no-verify`, no `LEFTHOOK=0`, no skipped commands. A failing hook means fix the cause, or stop and report the exact failure to the user.\n- Never merge a release-please PR. Leave it open until the user explicitly says to merge it.\n- Pin every GitHub Action, `actions/*` included, to a full commit SHA with the version in a trailing comment. Resolve with `gh api` at write time.\n- Lighthouse runs only through the chrome-devtools MCP `lighthouse_audit` tool against `pnpm preview`. Never add `@lhci/cli` or `lighthouse` to the repo in any form (`devDependencies`, `pnpm dlx`, CI, hooks): their dependency tree carries unpatched advisories.\n- Edit `README.md` only through the `readme-generator` skill.\n\nContents of /Users/anchildress1/.claude/projects/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/memory/MEMORY.md (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\n- [Volta + pnpm toolchain](volta-pnpm-toolchain.md) — pnpm 12 breaks Volta; VOLTA_FEATURE_PNPM=1 needed for pinned Node\n- [GCP secrets per project](gcp-secrets-per-project.md) — namespace secrets per app (GRAVESPEAKER_*); never reuse shared keys\n</system-reminder>"},{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is anchildress1@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n# gitStatus\nThis is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: test/benchmark-fixtures\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): main\n\nGit user: Ashley Childress\n\nStatus:\n(clean)\n\nRecent commits:\n60e756e test: mark the headstone answer key as human-reviewed\n138e1a1 chore: drop gitignore entries for hosts and files this repo never uses\n0a2fb9b build: keep benchmark fixtures out of the Docker build context\n2810dd1 docs: set fixture rules for the headstone benchmark\nc1cc170 test: add 9 headstone fixtures with ground truth for the benchmark\n\nClaude Code attached this context automatically; it isn't part of the user's message. It describes the user's own account and workspace, so they don't need it reported back.\n</system-reminder>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<system-reminder>\n<tools-used-before-summary>Artifact, ArtifactData, Bash, Edit, Read, Skill, ToolSearch, WebFetch, WebSearch, Write</tools-used-before-summary>\n</system-reminder>\nSummary:\n## 1. Primary Request and Intent\n\n**Project:** Gravespeaker, an MVP for Hacktoberfest 2026 \"Ghost in the Machine\". Hard deadline: 2026-10-31 23:59 PDT. Today is 2026-10-27.\n- User flow: photograph a headstone → AI extracts fields → search records with SerpApi → user confirms identity → grounded first-person narrative with citations.\n- Repo: `/Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker`. SvelteKit, Node 26.12.1, pnpm 11.28.9, Mastra, Gemma 4 through the Gemini API, Cloud Run deploy.\n\n**Requests over the session:**\n1. Start a new branch, install spec-kit (used going forward), and plan the build.\n   - Add a v2 PRD entry for searching socials for people who died after a cutoff year, via SerpApi, grounded in a search.\n   - That branch is spec-kit setup only. Feature builds go on new branches.\n2. Prettier config fix (approved).\n   - Pick the cutoff year: user accepted 2010.\n   - Get fixture headstones.\n   - Update CLAUDE.md for minimal output.\n3. Disallow famous people in fixtures.\n4. Find a pre-1900 illegible stone; set cutoff 2010; make atomic commits as you go; add `.semgrepignore`; remove `archbald-tomb`; focus on modern graves.\n5. Too many images. Focus is modern, meaning post-2010, graves. Set up an easy human review of the answer key.\n6. Manifest must hold the full stone text: `notes` becomes `text`, `people` unchanged.\n   - Fix history (fine if not pushed; only ask after pushing to GitHub).\n   - Delete dead code and config.\n7. Holmquist is an edge case to test. User then corrected me: they never said to exclude it from scoring.\n8. \"run the benchmark now\".\n9. **LATEST (in progress), verbatim:**\n   - \"make sure these results are stored in a file somewhere for reference during the post.\"\n   - \"note: the code must use mastra properly to retry as needed and handle errors. this is a given anyway\"\n   - \"holmquist which was a dual tombstone is now a non-goal. add TTD for v2 to the PRD that says we can tune the prompts then for that scenario\"\n   - \"take that one out and gemma 4 26b works and is hosted. is there a smaller hosted gemma? Cloud Run doesn't give me GPUs. Could we use render or digital ocean? if yes, test smaller gemma locally. this kills our open model requirement if flash is honestly that much better. only diff is that open ones are free with gemini key?? ground in a search.\"\n   - \"we need a deterministic layer in the code that parses names, strips/normalizes dates.\"\n   - \"languages other than english are also a non-goal for now, do not code against but we will not optimize for them\"\n\n## 2. Key Technical Concepts\n\n- **GitHub spec-kit v1.2.3**, installed via `uvx --from git+https://github.com/github/spec-kit.git@v1.2.3 specify init --here --force --integration claude --script sh --non-interactive --ignore-agent-tools`. Skills: `/speckit-specify` → `/speckit-plan` → `/speckit-tasks` → `/speckit-implement`.\n- **Gemini API models available with the project key:**\n  - Hosted Gemma: only `gemma-4-26b-a4b-it` and `gemma-4-31b-it`. No smaller hosted Gemma.\n  - Closed comparison model: `gemini-3.8-flash`.\n- **Gemma 4 family:** E2B, E4B, 26B A4B (3.8B active), 31B dense, and 12B Unified (added June 2026).\n- **Pricing (official page, fetched):**\n  - Gemma 4: free tier \"Free of charge\"; paid tier \"Not available\". So production runs on free-tier rate limits, and Google uses free-tier data to improve its products.\n  - gemini-3.8-flash paid: $0.75 input / $3.75 output per 1M tokens through 2026-12-31.\n- **Cloud Run DOES support GPUs:** NVIDIA L4 is GA with no quota request and 3 GPUs auto-granted. This contradicts the user's premise and must be pushed back on.\n- **Render / DigitalOcean:** the search returned nothing usable.\n- **Local hardware:** Apple M4 Max with 36 GB. Ollama 0.40.11 already has `gemma4:26b` and `gemma4:12b`; `llama-server` is also installed.\n- **SerpApi:**\n  - Google engine supports the `site:` operator.\n  - Facebook Profile API needs `profile_id` (no name search).\n  - An Instagram Profile engine exists. No LinkedIn engine.\n- **Wikimedia Commons:** API used for CC-licensed fixtures; Wikipedia API used for notability checks.\n- **Artifact** with `db` + `user` capabilities, used for the review page.\n\n## 3. Files and Code Sections\n\n**Branch `chore/speckit-setup`** (unpushed):\n- `e4bcc9e` build: install GitHub spec-kit 1.2.3 with the Claude integration. Adds `.claude/skills/speckit-*`, `.specify/`, and `/.specify/` in `.prettierignore` (its manifests hash the generated files).\n- `57c2b57` docs: ratify the Gravespeaker spec-kit constitution (`.specify/memory/constitution.md`).\n  - Gates: MVP scope, never fabricate history, never silently merge identities, two seams only, behavior-driven tests.\n  - Defers to CLAUDE.md and the PRD.\n- `e346e3a` docs: limit agent output to what the user must act on. Adds a `## Output` section to CLAUDE.md:\n  ```\n  ## Output\n  - The user designs; you implement. Report only what the user must act on or must know changed in the repo.\n  - Per change: file and one-line effect. No narration of steps, tools, or reasoning.\n  - Surface blockers, decisions needed, and failures in full. Omit everything else.\n  ```\n- `a1e8487` docs: add social media search for recent deaths to the v2 PRD. PRD §10 entry \"Social media search for recent deaths (key v2 feature)\":\n  - Death year 2010 or later.\n  - Pew stat: social networking among online adults 50+ reached 42% in 2010.\n  - SerpApi two-step: Google `site:` search, then the Facebook/Instagram profile engines.\n  - No LinkedIn engine.\n  - Profile hits go through normal identity verification.\n  - Living people's details stay out; link, don't republish.\n\n**Branch `test/benchmark-fixtures`** (current; from main; unpushed; history rewritten cleanly):\n- `c1cc170` test: add 9 headstone fixtures with ground truth for the benchmark.\n- `2810dd1` docs: set fixture rules for the headstone benchmark. CLAUDE.md Layout lines:\n  ```\n  - Benchmark script: `scripts/benchmark.ts`. Fixture images: `fixtures/`, ground truth in `fixtures/manifest.json`.\n  - Fixtures never depict notable people (anyone with a Wikipedia article). Models already know them, which inflates OCR and grounding scores. Same-name namesakes are allowed and noted as identity traps.\n  - Keep the fixture set small. Recent graves (death 2010+) are the baseline. Historic stones are a minority that stress-test reading, never the norm.\n  - Ground truth is unverified until the user reviews it (`reviewed: true`). Never publish benchmark numbers from unreviewed fixtures.\n  ```\n  This branch's CLAUDE.md does NOT have the Output section; that lives on the other branch.\n- `0a2fb9b` build: keep benchmark fixtures out of the Docker build context. Adds `fixtures` to `.dockerignore`.\n- `138e1a1` chore: drop gitignore entries for hosts and files this repo never uses. Removes `.output .vercel .netlify .wrangler` and `!.env.test`.\n- `60e756e` test: mark the headstone answer key as human-reviewed.\n  - User's own edits: all `reviewed: true`; Ellis text corrected to \"Tho,s\" and \"aged 11 years\".\n  - Trailer: `Commit-generated-by`.\n\n**`fixtures/manifest.json`** schema per entry:\n```\n{ file, era: \"recent\"|\"historic\", difficulty: \"clean\"|\"weathered\"|\"edge\"|\"illegible\", text: string|null (lines joined by \\n, [?] for unreadable word), people: [{name, born, died}] (dates \"YYYY\" or \"YYYY-MM-DD\", ? for unreadable digit, null if absent), source, author, license, licenseUrl, reviewed }\n```\n\n**Fixtures (9):**\n| File | Expected people | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| chambers-2011 | Corwin \"Cory\" Garner Chambers, 1959-11-07 to 2011-09-12 | |\n| gonzalez-2021 | Alfonso Gonzalez, 1951-11-18 to 2021-01-17 | Spanish |\n| guo-2025 | Jiunn-Shih Guo, 1937-01-18 to 2025-12-10 | Chinese 郭俊士 |\n| hildebrand-iii-2018 | Edward Hildebrand III, 1966 to 2018 | |\n| hildebrand-jr-2015 | Edward Leroy Hildebrand Jr, 1942-04-28 to 2015-10-10 | |\n| holmquist-2024 | Marsha Fox Holmquist, b. 1940-11-05; Charles Ray Holmquist, 1948-03-12 to 2024-05-06 | |\n| cunningham-1752 | Andrew Cunningham, d. 1752-04-27 | |\n| ellis-1818 | Betsey Ellis, d. 1818-07-28 | Zoom confirmed 1818 |\n| illegible-marble | none | text null |\n\n**Benchmark scratch scripts** (not in repo), in `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench/`:\n- `run.mjs`: REST `generateContent` via fetch, temperature 0, same PROMPT for all models, `AbortSignal.timeout(240_000)`, writes results after each call. Scores text by Levenshtein-normalized similarity; people by name match plus born/died.\n- `results.json`: the full hosted run.\n- `run-local.mjs`: Ollama `/api/chat` variant (`think: false`, images base64). Models come from the `MODELS` env var; holmquist excluded.\n- `results-local-12b.json`.\n- PROMPT (verbatim core):\n  ```\n  You are reading a photo of a gravestone for a historical research tool.\n  Transcribe ONLY what is carved or cast on the stone. Do NOT add, guess, or complete anything you cannot see.\n  Return ONLY a JSON object, no prose, no code fences:\n  {\"text\": string|null, \"people\": [{\"name\": string, \"born\": string|null, \"died\": string|null}]}\n  - text: every line of the inscription in order, joined with \"\\n\". Write \"[?]\" for a word you cannot read. null if nothing is legible.\n  - people: one entry per person commemorated. name exactly as carved. born/died as \"YYYY\" or \"YYYY-MM-DD\"; use \"?\" for an unreadable digit; null when the date is not on the stone. [] if no name is legible.\n  - A year that is not a birth or death (marriage length, erection date) is NOT a life date.\n  ```\n- **Rescoring logic** (Python, run inline): name tokens lowercased with non `[a-z0-9 ]` stripped (this drops CJK), \"m.r\" mapped to \"mr\", honorifics `mr/mrs/miss/dr` removed; tokens must match exactly. A person whose name doesn't match gets 0 for all three fields.\n\n**Review artifact:** https://claude.ai/artifact/2xtJSGENbbvQBMcHxyGKsZ\n- Source: `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/review/index.html`.\n- Uses `db` collection `reviews`, which is empty. The user reviewed in the IDE instead, so the page is obsolete; I offered deletion with no answer.\n\n## 4. Errors and fixes\n\n- **`specify init --here` refused a non-empty dir under `--non-interactive`:** added `--force`, after a scratch dry run confirmed it only writes `.claude/skills` and `.specify`.\n- **Prettier failed on `.specify` files:** added `/.specify/` to `.prettierignore` with user approval. Reformatting would break spec-kit's manifest hashes.\n- **Semgrep Guardian \"payload exceeded maximum size\":** Guardian scans files touched by tool calls (the JPGs) and ignores `.semgrepignore`. Nothing in the repo runs Semgrep, so `.semgrepignore` was dead and was removed during the history rewrite.\n- **Famous people in fixtures** (side-agent note; user said fix it): replaced with non-notables, checked via the Wikipedia API.\n- **Too many and too-old fixtures** (user feedback twice): trimmed to 9, post-2010 baseline.\n- **Answer key written by AI** (side agent): built the review page. The user reviewed in the manifest directly.\n- **`notes` → `text`:** user wanted the full inscription; replaced.\n- **Commit bullet wrap** in an old commit: resolved by the history rewrite.\n- **User correction:** I added `scored: false` for holmquist and a CLAUDE.md rule. User: \"I did not say to skip it in scoring. what i said was test it and if it doesn't work then we'll adjust the requirements\". Dropped that commit with `git reset --hard HEAD~1`; branch was unpushed and the tree clean.\n- **First benchmark run crashed:** `UND_ERR_HEADERS_TIMEOUT` from undici's 300 s default on a Gemma call, with no results saved. Added a per-call `AbortSignal.timeout(240_000)` with try/catch and incremental `writeFile`.\n- **Scoring too strict:** name compare was case-sensitive (models return CAPS). Rescored with normalized tokens.\n\n## 5. Problem Solving — benchmark results\n\n**Hosted run, all 9 fixtures (rescored):**\n| Model | Fields correct | Text similarity | Avg time | Timeouts |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| gemma-4-26b-a4b-it | 21/28 (75%) | 0.946 | 34.7 s | 0 |\n| gemma-4-31b-it | 18/25 (72%) | 0.941 | 55.0 s | 1 (cunningham) |\n| gemini-3.8-flash | 24/28 (86%) | 0.943 | 14.5 s | 0 |\n\n**Per-fixture findings:**\n- Holmquist: both Gemma models read every date right but left off the shared surname (0/6). Gemini scored 6/6.\n- Ellis: all three models read 1818 as 1848. Zoom confirmed 1818.\n- Gemini wrote \"LE ROY\" for LEROY on hildebrand-jr (0/3 under strict scoring; dates were right).\n\n**Excluding holmquist (not yet stated to the user):**\n- Gemma 26B: 21/22 (95%).\n- Gemma 31B: 18/19.\n- Gemini Flash: 18/22 (82%).\n- So Flash is NOT better; Gemma 26B leads. Speed is Gemma's only drawback. This contradicts the user's \"this kills our open model requirement\" premise.\n\n**Local Gemma 4 12B via Ollama (holmquist excluded), raw strict-score output:**\n- chambers 2/3 (likely a case issue), gonzalez 3/3, guo 3/3, hildebrand-iii 3/3, hildebrand-jr 2/3 (likely case), cunningham 0/3 with 1 extra (likely the \"Mr\" honorific), ellis text 0.74 and 0/3 with 1 extra, illegible 1/1.\n- About 4 s per image on the M4 Max.\n- Not yet rescored with the normalized scorer.\n\n**Other state:**\n- `ollama pull gemma4:e4b` running in background (task b5yjg8svu).\n- Conflict to flag: CLAUDE.md Scope says \"No feature flags, plugin systems, config layers, caching, retries, queues, or i18n unless a broken user path demands it.\" The user now requires Mastra retries and error handling. Update CLAUDE.md to allow Mastra's built-in retry and error handling (no hand-rolled retries) and tell the user about the change.\n\n## 6. All user messages\n\n1. \"Start a new branch. install speckit in this repo, we will use that going forward. read @docs/PRD.md and @CLAUDE.md. we need a plan of attack to actually accomplish this build in what little time we have to do it. Add to v2 PRD that we want to search socials for any person who died after XX year, which we will pick in addition to the gh search. This should be key feature and doable with SERPAPI. confirm with a grounded search. this branch will be speckit setup and init. feature builds will be on a new branch\"\n2. \"- yes to config for prettier. fix it. - help me pick a year that makes sense for the cutoff. - go get fixture headstones. we need to start there. i don't have time to find a cemetary rn. we can use good headstone pics, too. it should be a mix. - also update @CLAUDE.md to minimize output verbosity scoped only to what user needs to act on or needs to know about changes in the repo. the user designs. you code/implement.\"\n3. (Side-agent note about famous fixtures) \"fix this by disallowing famous people in the fixture for testing\"\n4. \"- search for early 19th century tombstone pics for can't read one - 2010 died after is fine - make atomic commits as you go - add semgrep ignore as suggested - archbald-tomb needs to go. too complex. - you also have all old and really old tombs. those are going to be hard to find and are the exception we need to test for reading markers, not the baseline for how the app functions. we're shooting for modern graves that must be present in the fixture test. keep the best old ones, some legible some not, but focus on modern\"\n5. \"this seems like a fuck ton of images for a \"small benchmark\" to see if gemma can read them cleanly like the PRD says. Especially when our focus is on modern aka post-2010 grave markers.\"\n6. (Mid-turn, after side-agent note about the AI-written answer key) \"we're going to fix this once I'm happy with the pics. you need to set this up so i can easily review. make corrections as needed or simply state illegible.\"\n7. \"now we're testing the ability of the llm to read the image, right? doesn't it make sense that you'd put in the manifest the full text from the gravestone? the llm also needs to pick the persons full name, birthdate, and deceased date. you've got these already. don't change it. notes should become full text though. - fix the history, fine if not pushed yet. you only have to ask after pushing to gh - delete dead code/config\"\n8. \"@fixtures/holmquist-2024.jpg this is an edge case. we'll run a test if the model can handle dual plots like this, but it is a non-goal if it cannot. we're not going to waste time on that. otherwise all are reviewed by me and marked true in manifest\"\n9. \"I did not say to skip it in scoring. what i said was test it and if it doesn't work then we'll adjust the requirements\"\n10. \"run the benchmark now\"\n11. (Latest) \"- make sure these results are stored in a file somewhere for reference during the post. - note: the code must use mastra properly to retry as needed and handle errors. this is a given anyway - holmquist which was a dual tombstone is now a non-goal. add TTD for v2 to the PRD that says we can tune the prompts then for that scenario - take that one out and gemma 4 26b works and is hosted. is there a smaller hosted gemma? Cloud Run doesn't give me GPUs. Could we use render or digital ocean? if yes, test smaller gemma locally. this kills our open model requirement if flash is honestly that much better. only diff is that open ones are free with gemini key?? ground in a search. - we need a deterministic layer in the code that parses names, strips/normalizes dates. - languages other than english are also a non-goal for now, do not code against but we will not optimize for them\"\n\n**Standing constraints, verbatim, from CLAUDE.md and the user's global rules:**\n- \"Never place secrets outside: a local `.env` file, or a secure vault explicitly chosen by the user.\"\n- \"Never print secret values. Pipe from `.env` into `gcloud secrets create --data-file=-`.\" (`.env` holds `GEMINI_API_KEY` and `SERPAPI_API_KEY`; loaded with `set -a && . ./.env`; never echo the values.)\n- \"Secret Manager bills per secret. Create one only when prod cannot run without it. Ask before adding any.\"\n- \"Never `git push` unless the user asks for it in that message.\"\n- \"All commits must be signed with a GPG key.\"\n- \"Commits never land directly on `main`.\"\n- \"Every AI-authored commit must include a `Generated-by` footer\"; commitlint also requires `Signed-off-by: Ashley Childress <anchildress1@gmail.com>`.\n- \"Never bypass Lefthook on commit or push: no `--no-verify`, no `LEFTHOOK=0`.\"\n- \"You may **not** modify repository configuration files unless explicitly instructed.\"\n- \"Never merge a release-please PR.\"\n- \"Lighthouse runs only through the chrome-devtools MCP ... Never add `@lhci/cli` or `lighthouse` to the repo.\"\n- \"Edit `README.md` only through the `readme-generator` skill.\"\n- \"Pin every GitHub Action ... to a full commit SHA.\"\n- \"Port kills: use `kill <pid>` only.\"\n- \"Mock at the network boundary (Gemini API, SerpApi). Never hit live APIs in tests.\"\n- \"Validation gate before reporting any change as done: `make ai-checks`.\"\n- History rewrites are OK before pushing; ask only after pushing to GitHub.\n- Use the generate-commit-message skill; atomic commits as you go.\n\n## 7. Pending Tasks (from the latest message)\n\n1. **Store the benchmark results in the repo** for the DEV post: e.g. `docs/benchmarks/` with a human-readable markdown summary plus the raw JSON (hosted + local). Commit.\n2. **Mastra retries and error handling:** record as a requirement and resolve the CLAUDE.md \"no retries\" conflict (Mastra built-in only, no hand-rolled). Flag the change to the user.\n3. **Holmquist / multi-person stones:**\n   - Mark as non-goal in the PRD.\n   - Add a v2 PRD entry (user wrote \"TTD\", read as TODO) to tune prompts for companion/dual stones then.\n   - \"take that one out\": remove `holmquist-2024` from fixtures and the manifest.\n4. **Answer the hosting questions, grounded:**\n   - No smaller hosted Gemma on the Gemini API (only 26B A4B and 31B).\n   - Cloud Run DOES have L4 GPUs (GA). Push back on the user's premise.\n   - Render / DigitalOcean: unverified, but unnecessary.\n   - Gemma 4 on the Gemini API is free-tier only; paid tier not available. Risks: rate limits and Google training on the data.\n   - Excluding holmquist, Gemma 26B 95% beats Flash 82%, so the open-model requirement survives; latency (~35 s vs ~15 s) is the real cost.\n   - PRD says no self-hosting unless benchmarks justify it.\n5. **Local smaller Gemma test:**\n   - Rescore the 12B results with the normalized scorer.\n   - Run E4B once the pull finishes: `MODELS=gemma4:e4b node $B/run-local.mjs \"$PWD/fixtures\" $B/results-local-e4b.json`.\n   - Report the comparison.\n6. **Deterministic layer for name parsing and date normalization:** record as a requirement (PRD/CLAUDE.md). Build it in the feature branch via spec-kit, not now.\n7. **Non-English languages:** add as a non-goal in the PRD (don't code against them; don't optimize for them).\n8. Still open: offer to delete the obsolete review artifact (no reply yet). `scripts/benchmark.ts` (the real Mastra-based benchmark) still needs building via spec-kit on a feature branch.\n\n## 8. Current Work\n\nWorking on the user's latest multi-part message:\n- Grounding searches are done:\n  - Gemma 4 sizes.\n  - Cloud Run GPU is GA.\n  - Pricing: Gemma 4 free-only, paid \"Not available\"; 3.8 Flash $0.75/$3.75.\n  - Render/DO search failed.\n- Hosted Gemma list confirmed: only 26b-a4b and 31b.\n- Local Ollama test of `gemma4:12b` ran on 8 fixtures (holmquist excluded) in about 4 s each; raw strict-score output is listed in section 5.\n- `ollama pull gemma4:e4b` was started in the background (task b5yjg8svu) and has not finished.\n- No repo edits yet for the latest message.\n- Current branch: `test/benchmark-fixtures`, clean, unpushed.\n\n## 9. Optional Next Step\n\nContinue the latest request: \"take that one out and gemma 4 26b works and is hosted. is there a smaller hosted gemma? ... if yes, test smaller gemma locally ... ground in a search.\"\n\n1. Rescore `results-local-12b.json` with the normalized scorer.\n2. When the E4B pull finishes, run `MODELS=gemma4:e4b node $B/run-local.mjs \"$PWD/fixtures\" $B/results-local-e4b.json` and rescore.\n3. Then, on `test/benchmark-fixtures`, with `make ai-checks` and one atomic signed commit per change:\n   - Remove the holmquist fixture.\n   - Write the results doc plus raw JSON under `docs/benchmarks/` (\"make sure these results are stored in a file somewhere for reference during the post\").\n   - Update the PRD: multi-person stones as non-goal plus v2 prompt-tuning TODO; non-English as non-goal; deterministic name/date normalization layer; Mastra retry/error handling.\n   - Update the CLAUDE.md retries line so Mastra built-in retries are allowed.\n4. Report the grounded hosting answer with source links:\n   - Cloud Run GPUs exist (https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/serverless/cloud-run-gpus-are-now-generally-available, https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/services/gpu).\n   - Gemma 4 is free-tier only (https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing).\n   - Gemma 4 sizes (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/releases).\n   - Gemma 26B beats Flash once holmquist is excluded.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/anchildress1/.claude/projects/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"56f61af43fffe8a5d51767419c93d33ee34c32e1cef879c68060c9cc97049d2e","message":{"role":"system","content":"Today's date is 2026-10-27.","output_config":{"effort":"medium"}}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"d4074d16cc32e156fd6e829c1ad3cb87b9f77d2703dc7ebe499ea43b5cf8d182","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016CfySGdVtgrQednsm4tpB2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench/rescore.py","content":"\"\"\"Rescore saved model answers: case/punctuation-insensitive names, honorifics and non-Latin script ignored.\"\"\"\nimport json, re, sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nmanifest = {e[\"file\"]: e for e in json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))}\nskip = set(sys.argv[2].split(\",\")) if sys.argv[2] else set()\nrows = [x for p in sys.argv[3:] for x in json.load(open(p)) if x[\"file\"] not in skip]\n\n\ndef toks(s):\n    t = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9 ]+\", \" \", (s or \"\").lower().replace(\"m.r\", \"mr\")).split()\n    return [x for x in t if x not in {\"mr\", \"mrs\", \"miss\", \"dr\"}]\n\n\nagg = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(float))\ncells = {}\nfor x in rows:\n    f, a = manifest[x[\"file\"]], agg[x[\"model\"]]\n    a[\"calls\"] += 1\n    a[\"ms\"] += x[\"ms\"]\n    if x.get(\"error\"):\n        a[\"errors\"] += 1\n        cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = \"timeout\" if \"Timeout\" in x[\"error\"] else \"error\"\n        continue\n    a[\"text\"] += x[\"text\"]\n    got = list(x[\"got\"].get(\"people\") or [])\n    if not f[\"people\"]:\n        ok = 0 if got else 1\n        a[\"fields\"] += ok\n        a[\"total\"] += 1\n        cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = f\"{ok}/1\"\n        continue\n    c = t = 0\n    for p in f[\"people\"]:\n        q = next((q for q in got if toks(q.get(\"name\")) == toks(p[\"name\"])), None)\n        if q:\n            got.remove(q)\n        for k in (\"name\", \"born\", \"died\"):\n            t += 1\n            c += bool(q) and (k == \"name\" or (q.get(k) or None) == (p[k] or None))\n    a[\"fields\"] += c\n    a[\"total\"] += t\n    cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = f\"{c}/{t}\"\n\nmodels = list(agg)\nfiles = [fn for fn in manifest if fn not in skip]\nprint(\"| Fixture | \" + \" | \".join(models) + \" |\")\nprint(\"|---|\" + \"---|\" * len(models))\nfor fn in files:\n    print(f\"| {fn.removesuffix('.jpg')} | \" + \" | \".join(cells.get((fn, m), \"-\") for m in models) + \" |\")\nprint(\"| **Fields correct** | \" + \" | \".join(f\"**{int(a['fields'])}/{int(a['total'])} ({a['fields']/a['total']:.0%})**\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\nprint(\"| Text similarity | \" + \" | \".join(f\"{a['text']/(a['calls']-a['errors']):.1%}\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\nprint(\"| Timeouts | \" + \" | \".join(str(int(a[\"errors\"])) for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\nprint(\"| Avg time per photo | \" + \" | \".join(f\"{a['ms']/a['calls']/1000:.1f} s\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012AeGrALdK3EDPQn3qfQ6GW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"B=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench; M=/Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker/fixtures/manifest.json; python3 -I $B/rescore.py $M holmquist-2024.jpg $B/results.json $B/results-local-12b.json; python3 -I -c 'import json;[print(x[\"model\"],x[\"file\"],json.dumps(x[\"got\"][\"people\"])) for x in json.load(open(\"'$B'/results-local-12b.json\")) if x[\"file\"] in (\"ellis-1818.jpg\",\"cunningham-1752.jpg\")]'","description":"Score hosted and local runs without the dual stone"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"ba06a6bec69a0cda66800a9b60b528929fc4c0289fb3cf775c68acf54205cd8c","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CfySGdVtgrQednsm4tpB2","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench/rescore.py (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012AeGrALdK3EDPQn3qfQ6GW","type":"tool_result","content":"| Fixture | gemma-4-26b-a4b-it | gemma-4-31b-it | gemini-3.8-flash | gemma4:12b |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| chambers-2011 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 |\n| gonzalez-2021 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 |\n| guo-2025 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 |\n| hildebrand-iii-2018 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 3/3 |\n| hildebrand-jr-2015 | 3/3 | 3/3 | 0/3 | 3/3 |\n| cunningham-1752 | 3/3 | timeout | 3/3 | 0/3 |\n| ellis-1818 | 2/3 | 2/3 | 2/3 | 0/3 |\n| illegible-marble | 1/1 | 1/1 | 1/1 | 1/1 |\n| **Fields correct** | **21/22 (95%)** | **18/19 (95%)** | **18/22 (82%)** | **16/22 (73%)** |\n| Text similarity | 99.4% | 99.3% | 99.1% | 96.6% |\n| Timeouts | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 |\n| Avg time per photo | 24.5 s | 50.1 s | 11.9 s | 5.0 s |\ngemma4:12b cunningham-1752.jpg [{\"name\": \"M. ANDREW CUNNINGHAM\", \"born\": null, \"died\": \"1752\"}]\ngemma4:12b ellis-1818.jpg [{\"name\": \"BETSEY BUCKS\", \"born\": null, \"died\": \"1810\"}]","is_error":false},{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nAttribution for git commits and pull requests you create from here on (this replaces Claude Code's own earlier attribution guidance, such as a previous copy of this reminder; the user's own instructions about these lines, such as a CLAUDE.md or memory rule, take precedence over this reminder, but do not add attribution lines this reminder leaves out):\n- End git commit messages with:\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n- End pull request descriptions with:\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13","hash":"946ba56954edf70268bff486253c4000ceb2f95886388ee31489bcc0d7897314","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Concise output style is active. Be concise: lead with the result, skip preamble and narration, keep only what the user needs.\n\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench/rescore.py\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Rescore saved model answers: case/punctuation-insensitive names, honorifics and non-Latin script ignored.\"\"\"\n2\timport json, re, sys\n3\tfrom collections import defaultdict\n4\t\n5\tmanifest = {e[\"file\"]: e for e in json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))}\n6\tskip = set(sys.argv[2].split(\",\")) if sys.argv[2] else set()\n7\trows = [x for p in sys.argv[3:] for x in json.load(open(p)) if x[\"file\"] not in skip]\n8\t\n9\t\n10\tdef toks(s):\n11\t    t = re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9 ]+\", \" \", (s or \"\").lower().replace(\"m.r\", \"mr\")).split()\n12\t    return [x for x in t if x not in {\"mr\", \"mrs\", \"miss\", \"dr\"}]\n13\t\n14\t\n15\tagg = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(float))\n16\tcells = {}\n17\tfor x in rows:\n18\t    f, a = manifest[x[\"file\"]], agg[x[\"model\"]]\n19\t    a[\"calls\"] += 1\n20\t    a[\"ms\"] += x[\"ms\"]\n21\t    if x.get(\"error\"):\n22\t        a[\"errors\"] += 1\n23\t        cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = \"timeout\" if \"Timeout\" in x[\"error\"] else \"error\"\n24\t        continue\n25\t    a[\"text\"] += x[\"text\"]\n26\t    got = list(x[\"got\"].get(\"people\") or [])\n27\t    if not f[\"people\"]:\n28\t        ok = 0 if got else 1\n29\t        a[\"fields\"] += ok\n30\t        a[\"total\"] += 1\n31\t        cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = f\"{ok}/1\"\n32\t        continue\n33\t    c = t = 0\n34\t    for p in f[\"people\"]:\n35\t        q = next((q for q in got if toks(q.get(\"name\")) == toks(p[\"name\"])), None)\n36\t        if q:\n37\t            got.remove(q)\n38\t        for k in (\"name\", \"born\", \"died\"):\n39\t            t += 1\n40\t            c += bool(q) and (k == \"name\" or (q.get(k) or None) == (p[k] or None))\n41\t    a[\"fields\"] += c\n42\t    a[\"total\"] += t\n43\t    cells[(x[\"file\"], x[\"model\"])] = f\"{c}/{t}\"\n44\t\n45\tmodels = list(agg)\n46\tfiles = [fn for fn in manifest if fn not in skip]\n47\tprint(\"| Fixture | \" + \" | \".join(models) + \" |\")\n48\tprint(\"|---|\" + \"---|\" * len(models))\n49\tfor fn in files:\n50\t    print(f\"| {fn.removesuffix('.jpg')} | \" + \" | \".join(cells.get((fn, m), \"-\") for m in models) + \" |\")\n51\tprint(\"| **Fields correct** | \" + \" | \".join(f\"**{int(a['fields'])}/{int(a['total'])} ({a['fields']/a['total']:.0%})**\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\n52\tprint(\"| Text similarity | \" + \" | \".join(f\"{a['text']/(a['calls']-a['errors']):.1%}\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\n53\tprint(\"| Timeouts | \" + \" | \".join(str(int(a[\"errors\"])) for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\n54\tprint(\"| Avg time per photo | \" + \" | \".join(f\"{a['ms']/a['calls']/1000:.1f} s\" for a in agg.values()) + \" |\")\n55\t\n\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/bench/run.mjs\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t// Throwaway OCR smoke run: same prompt and images for every model, scored against fixtures/manifest.json.\n2\timport { readFile, writeFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n3\t\n4\tconst [fixturesDir, outFile] = process.argv.slice(2);\n5\tconst KEY = process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY;\n6\tconst MODELS = [\"gemma-4-26b-a4b-it\", \"gemma-4-31b-it\", \"gemini-3.8-flash\"];\n7\t\n8\tconst PROMPT = `You are reading a photo of a gravestone for a historical research tool.\n9\t\n10\tTranscribe ONLY what is carved or cast on the stone. Do NOT add, guess, or complete anything you cannot see.\n11\t\n12\tReturn ONLY a JSON object, no prose, no code fences:\n13\t{\"text\": string|null, \"people\": [{\"name\": string, \"born\": string|null, \"died\": string|null}]}\n14\t\n15\t- text: every line of the inscription in order, joined with \"\\\\n\". Write \"[?]\" for a word you cannot read. null if nothing is legible.\n16\t- people: one entry per person commemorated. name exactly as carved. born/died as \"YYYY\" or \"YYYY-MM-DD\"; use \"?\" for an unreadable digit; null when the date is not on the stone. [] if no name is legible.\n17\t- A year that is not a birth or death (marriage length, erection date) is NOT a life date.`;\n18\t\n19\tconst norm = (s) => (s ?? \"\").toLowerCase().replace(/[^\\p{L}\\p{N}?]+/gu, \" \").trim();\n20\t\n21\tfunction lev(a, b) {\n22\t  const d = Array.from({ length: a.length + 1 }, (_, i) => [i]);\n23\t  for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++) d[0][j] = j;\n24\t  for (let i = 1; i <= a.length; i++)\n25\t    for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++)\n26\t      d[i][j] = Math.min(d[i - 1][j] + 1, d[i][j - 1] + 1, d[i - 1][j - 1] + (a[i - 1] === b[j - 1] ? 0 : 1));\n27\t  return d[a.length][b.length];\n28\t}\n29\t\n30\tfunction textScore(expected, got) {\n31\t  const e = norm(expected), g = norm(got);\n32\t  if (!e) return g ? 0 : 1;\n33\t  return Math.max(0, 1 - lev(e, g) / e.length);\n34\t}\n35\t\n36\tfunction peopleScore(expected, got) {\n37\t  if (!expected.length) return { fields: got.length ? 0 : 1, total: 1, correct: got.length ? 0 : 1, extra: got.length };\n38\t  let correct = 0, total = 0;\n39\t  const pool = [...got];\n40\t  for (const p of expected) {\n41\t    const i = pool.findIndex((q) => norm(q.name) === norm(p.name));\n42\t    const q = i >= 0 ? pool.splice(i, 1)[0] : null;\n43\t    for (const k of [\"name\", \"born\", \"died\"]) {\n44\t      total++;\n45\t      if (q && (q[k] ?? null) === (p[k] ?? null)) correct++;\n46\t    }\n47\t  }\n48\t  return { correct, total, extra: pool.length };\n49\t}\n50\t\n51\tasync function call(model, img) {\n52\t  const t0 = performance.now();\n53\t  let res, body;\n54\t  try {\n55\t    res = await fetch(`https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/${model}:generateContent`, {\n56\t      method: \"POST\",\n57\t      headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\", \"x-goog-api-key\": KEY },\n58\t      body: JSON.stringify({\n59\t        contents: [{ role: \"user\", parts: [{ inline_data: { mime_type: \"image/jpeg\", data: img } }, { text: PROMPT }] }],\n60\t        generationConfig: { temperature: 0 },\n61\t      }),\n62\t      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(240_000),\n63\t    });\n64\t    body = await res.json();\n65\t  } catch (e) {\n66\t    return { ms: Math.round(performance.now() - t0), error: `request failed: ${e.name}` };\n67\t  }\n68\t  const ms = Math.round(performance.now() - t0);\n69\t  if (!res.ok) return { ms, error: `${res.status} ${body.error?.message ?? \"\"}`.slice(0, 200) };\n70\t  const raw = (body.candidates?.[0]?.content?.parts ?? []).filter((p) => !p.thought).map((p) => p.text ?? \"\").join(\"\");\n71\t  const usage = body.usageMetadata ?? {};\n72\t  const json = raw.slice(raw.indexOf(\"{\"), raw.lastIndexOf(\"}\") + 1);\n73\t  try {\n74\t    return { ms, parsed: JSON.parse(json), raw, inTok: usage.promptTokenCount, outTok: (usage.candidatesTokenCount ?? 0) + (usage.thoughtsTokenCount ?? 0) };\n75\t  } catch {\n76\t    return { ms, error: \"unparseable JSON\", raw };\n77\t  }\n78\t}\n79\t\n80\tconst manifest = JSON.parse(await readFile(`${fixturesDir}/manifest.json`, \"utf8\"));\n81\tconst results = [];\n82\tfor (const f of manifest) {\n83\t  const img = (await readFile(`${fixturesDir}/${f.file}`)).toString(\"base64\");\n84\t  for (const model of MODELS) {\n85\t    const r = await call(model, img);\n86\t    const row = { file: f.file, era: f.era, model, ms: r.ms, inTok: r.inTok, outTok: r.outTok, error: r.error, got: r.parsed, raw: r.error ? r.raw : undefined };\n87\t    if (r.parsed) {\n88\t      row.text = textScore(f.text, r.parsed.text);\n89\t      Object.assign(row, peopleScore(f.people, Array.isArray(r.parsed.people) ? r.parsed.people : []));\n90\t    }\n91\t    results.push(row);\n92\t    await writeFile(outFile, JSON.stringify(results, null, 2));\n93\t    console.error(`${f.file.padEnd(26)} ${model.padEnd(20)} ${r.error ? \"ERR \" + r.error : `text ${row.text.toFixed(2)} fields ${row.correct}/${row.total} extra ${row.extra} ${r.ms}ms`}`);\n94\t  }\n95\t}\n96\tawait writeFile(outFile, JSON.stringify(results, null, 2));\n97\t\n\nNote: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/a78fbb29-c412-483a-b679-0722adb27f13/scratchpad/review/index.html was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nNote: /private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities/0.2.91/db.d.ts was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/anchildress1/git_personal/gravespeaker/docs/PRD.md\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# 🪦 Gravespeaker\n2\t\n3\t**Every stone has a story. Let it speak.**\n4\t\n5\t_Photograph a headstone. Discover a life. Hear the story the records left behind._\n6\t\n7\t**Project:** Hacktoberfest 2026, Week 4: Ghost in the Machine\n8\t**Deadline:** October 31, 2026, 11:59 PM PDT\n9\t**Status:** MVP in development\n10\t\n11\t## 1. Vision\n12\t\n13\tGravespeaker is a mobile-friendly web application that brings historical lives back into focus.\n14\t\n15\tUsers photograph a gravestone or upload an existing image. AI extracts identifying details, searches publicly accessible historical records, and reconstructs the deceased person's story in first person, as though they're speaking from beyond the grave.\n16\t\n17\tThe experience should feel mysterious and personal while remaining historically responsible.\n18\t\n19\t**Core principle:** Resurrect the story, not invent the person.\n20\t\n21\t## 2. MVP User Journey\n22\t\n23\t| #   | Step                  | What happens                                                                             | Done when                                                                                      |\n24\t| --- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n25\t| 1   | **Capture or upload** | Photograph a gravestone or select an existing image.                                     | Works from a phone camera and from a desktop file picker.                                      |\n26\t| 2   | **Identify**          | AI extracts names, dates, inscriptions and other useful details.                         | User can review and correct every extracted field before searching.                            |\n27\t| 3   | **Research**          | Search obituaries, cemetery records, historical archives and indexed public information. | Results come back with source URLs attached.                                                   |\n28\t| 4   | **Verify**            | Cross-reference names, dates and places.                                                 | When more than one plausible person matches, the user picks one (or none).                     |\n29\t| 5   | **Resurrect**         | Generate a short first-person narrative grounded in the confirmed records.               | Every factual sentence links to at least one source.                                           |\n30\t| 6   | **Explore**           | Show source links and separate documented facts from AI reconstruction.                  | Facts and reconstruction are visually distinct, and the narrative is labelled as AI-generated. |\n31\t\n32\tIf credible information can't be found, the app says so. It never fills historical gaps with fabricated claims.\n33\t\n34\t## 3. Technical Direction\n35\t\n36\t| Component            | Choice                                                                                                        |\n37\t| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n38\t| App                  | SvelteKit (Vite) as one app: UI plus server endpoints, built with `adapter-node`                              |\n39\t| Runtime              | Node.js 26.12.1 / TypeScript                                                                                  |\n40\t| AI orchestration     | Mastra, running inside the SvelteKit server endpoints                                                         |\n41\t| Vision and OCR       | Gemma 4 (`gemma-4-26b-a4b-it` / `gemma-4-31b-it`) via the Gemini API, authenticated with an AI Studio API key |\n42\t| Historical search    | SerpApi                                                                                                       |\n43\t| Narrative generation | Gemma 4                                                                                                       |\n44\t| Narration (stretch)  | ElevenLabs                                                                                                    |\n45\t| Hosting              | Google Cloud Run, one service, with API keys in Secret Manager                                                |\n46\t| Builder              | Claude Code                                                                                                   |\n47\t| Development tracking | Entire                                                                                                        |\n48\t\n49\tModel inference and historical search sit behind replaceable interfaces. Everything else stays concrete.\n50\t\n51\tNo self-hosted models unless benchmarks show a compelling advantage.\n52\t\n53\t## 4. Key Engineering Challenges\n54\t\n55\t**Headstone recognition**\n56\t\n57\t- Weathered stone, damaged inscriptions, shadows, unusual typography and partial dates.\n58\t- Measure actual recognition accuracy instead of assuming a vision model performs well.\n59\t\n60\t**Historical identity resolution**\n61\t\n62\t- Common names, incomplete records, contradictory dates and similarly named people.\n63\t- Use multiple identity signals (name, birth/death dates, location) and make uncertainty explicit.\n64\t- Never silently combine different people's histories.\n65\t\n66\t**Source discovery and reliability**\n67\t\n68\t- Public availability doesn't guarantee unrestricted reuse.\n69\t- Respect access restrictions, provenance, licensing and unavailable records. Link to sources; don't republish their content.\n70\t- Every substantive historical claim must trace back to supporting evidence.\n71\t\n72\t**First-person storytelling**\n73\t\n74\t- Atmospheric and engaging without inventing relationships, events, memories, thoughts or emotions.\n75\t- Clearly label narratives as AI-generated historical reconstructions.\n76\t- Leave out unnecessary sensitive details about living people (for example, surviving relatives named in obituaries).\n77\t\n78\t**Model selection**\n79\t\n80\t- Benchmark Gemma 4 against a closed Gemini model on identical test inputs.\n81\t- Compare OCR accuracy, factual grounding, unsupported claims, latency and cost.\n82\t- Consider a specialised open OCR model only if testing justifies the extra integration.\n83\t\n84\t## 5. Open Innovation and Challenge Alignment\n85\t\n86\tThe open-weight model has to meaningfully contribute to the finished app, not exist only for challenge eligibility.\n87\t\n88\t**Thesis for the DEV post:**\n89\t\n90\t> The value of open innovation isn't that we refused to use closed AI. It's that we could independently test, choose, and improve the technology instead of accepting one provider's answer as the only option.\n91\t\n92\tDocument where the open approach performs better, matches a closed alternative, or exposes tradeoffs. Back conclusions with real benchmarks and reproducible examples.\n93\t\n94\tThe post focuses on the problem, engineering decisions, real discoveries, failure cases, and the value of making forgotten histories more accessible.\n95\t\n96\t## 6. Scope Boundaries\n97\t\n98\t**Required**\n99\t\n100\t- Functional mobile-friendly website\n101\t- Camera and image upload\n102\t- AI inscription extraction with user correction\n103\t- Public-record search and identity verification\n104\t- Source-grounded first-person biography\n105\t- Clickable citations\n106\t- Small reproducible model evaluation: `scripts/benchmark.ts` runs Gemma 4 and a closed Gemini model on the same fixture images and records OCR accuracy, grounding, unsupported claims, latency and cost\n107\t- Deployed public demo\n108\t\n109\t**Stretch**\n110\t\n111\t- ElevenLabs voice narration, with a basic male and female voice\n112\t  - The AI picks the voice from obituary evidence first (pronouns, relationship words such as \"wife\" or \"son\"), then the name.\n113\t  - The guess is shown with the extracted fields, and the user can switch it like any other correction.\n114\t  - The guess only selects a voice; it is never stated as a historical fact in the story.\n115\t- Additional historical record providers\n116\t- Specialized OCR model fallback\n117\t- Enhanced atmospheric animations\n118\t\n119\t**Explicitly excluded**\n120\t\n121\t- User accounts or authentication\n122\t- Family trees and genealogical graph exploration\n123\t- Cemetery navigation and maps\n124\t- Native Android or iOS applications\n125\t- Custom model hosting\n126\t- Persistent user profiles and photo libraries\n127\t- Paywalled or login-gated record sources\n128\t\n129\t## 7. Privacy\n130\t\n131\t- Uploaded images are processed in memory and never stored.\n132\t- API keys stay server-side. The browser never calls a model or search provider directly.\n133\t\n134\t## 8. Schedule\n135\t\n136\t| Date            | Focus                                             | Milestone                        |\n137\t| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n138\t| **Tue, Oct 27** | Repo, strict `CLAUDE.md`, Entire setup, scaffold  | Development foundation           |\n139\t| **Wed, Oct 28** | Upload/camera, Gemma vision, extracted fields     | Working headstone identification |\n140\t| **Thu, Oct 29** | SerpApi, Mastra workflow, identity matching       | Working historical research      |\n141\t| **Fri, Oct 30** | Narrative, citations, model comparison, Cloud Run | Public end-to-end demo           |\n142\t| **Sat, Oct 31** | DEV post, screenshots, final verification         | Challenge submitted              |\n143\t\n144\tThe schedule leaves room for GitHub Universe. Prefer vertical slices, with a deployable app at every milestone. Saturday is for documentation and submission, not essential development.\n145\t\n146\t## 9. Definition of Done\n147\t\n148\tA user can visit Gravespeaker on a phone, photograph or upload a real gravestone, confirm the extracted identity, and receive a readable first-person historical reconstruction with accessible source evidence.\n149\t\n150\tIf the history can't be reliably established, the app says so clearly.\n151\t\n152\tThe project includes a working public demo, documented technical decisions, a meaningful open-versus-closed model comparison, and an engaging DEV submission.\n153\t\n154\t**Priority:** Complete one historically defensible end-to-end experience before adding features.\n155\t\n156\t## 10. v2 (after the challenge)\n157\t\n158\tNot part of the October 31 build. Captured so the ideas aren't lost.\n159\t\n160\t**Obituary photos during narration**\n161\t\n162\t- When the matched obituary has a photo, show it while the speaker is talking.\n163\t- Display it from its original URL with a visible credit and link. Never download, cache or re-host it, and skip any image whose source restricts embedding.\n164\t- This is the one deliberate exception to the \"link, don't republish\" rule in §4, so it needs a licensing check before it ships.\n165\t\n\nBackground shell b5yjg8svu (\"Download Gemma 4 E4B for a local test\") is still running (command, shown on one line: `ollama pull gemma4:e4b 2>&1 | tail -2`). Do not start it again; to restart it, stop it with TaskStop first. You can read its output at /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-anchildress1-git-personal-gravespeaker/e8fe1454-894d-486c-ada2-536b95330a4e/tasks/b5yjg8svu.output.\n\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: gemini-prompting\nPath: userSettings:gemini-prompting\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/anchildress1/.claude/skills/gemini-prompting\n\nSnapshot verified 2026-09-04 against <https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models>. Gemini API\nnames churn fast — `gemini-3.5-flash` went from default workhorse to legacy baseline in one\nquarter. Re-check the docs before writing a model string, and always when the current date is\nmore than ~3 months past the date above.\n\n## Models\n\n### Text and multimodal (GA)\n\n| API name | Tier | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `gemini-3.8-flash` | Flash | **Default workhorse.** Most intelligent Flash; software engineering and agents. |\n| `gemini-3.7-flash` | Flash | Previous generation. Complex coding. Prefer 3.8 for new work. |\n| `gemini-3.6-flash` | Flash | Balanced speed and multimodal. |\n| `gemini-3.5-flash` | Flash | **Legacy baseline.** High-throughput only. Do not start here. |\n| `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` | Flash-Lite | **Fastest and cheapest.** Narrow classification, boolean extraction. |\n| `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` | Flash-Lite | Frontier performance at lower cost. |\n| `gemini-2.5-pro` | Pro | Hardest multi-step reasoning where accuracy outweighs latency and cost. |\n| `gemini-2.5-flash` / `gemini-2.5-flash-lite` | 2.5 | Older line. New work belongs on 3.x. |\n\n### Preview — text and agentic\n\n| API name | Use it for |\n|---|---|\n| `gemini-3.1-pro-preview` | Advanced intelligence and reasoning. Pro tier — slower and pricier than Flash; justify it on the critical path. |\n| `gemini-3-flash-preview` | Frontier performance tier. |\n\n### Audio and speech\n\n| API name | Status | API | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `gemini-3.5-transcribe` | GA | `generateContent` | Speech-to-text with diarization. Batch. |\n| `gemini-3.5-transcribe-live` | GA | Live | Streaming speech-to-text. |\n| `gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview` | Preview | `generateContent` | Low-latency TTS with expressive control. |\n| `gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts` | Preview | `generateContent` | Controllable TTS. |\n| `gemini-2.5-pro-preview-tts` | Preview | `generateContent` | High-fidelity synthesis — podcasts, audiobooks. |\n| `gemini-3.1-flash-live-preview` | Preview | **Live only** | Low-latency audio-to-audio dialogue. |\n| `gemini-3.5-live-translate-preview` | Preview | **Live only** | Real-time speech-to-speech translation, 70+ languages. |\n| `gemini-2.5-flash-native-audio-preview-12-2025` | Preview | **Live only** | Real-time conversational agents. |\n\n**Check the API column before you pick.** Models marked *Live only* run over the Live API's\nstateful WebSocket session. They do **not** work with `generateContent` or `batchGenerateContent`,\nand they are speech-**to-speech**: audio in, audio out. They cannot serve an asynchronous\nrequest/response pipeline, and they cannot return structured text. Reaching for one in a batch\njob is a rewrite, not a config change.\n\n`gemini-3.5-live-translate-preview` is the most common mistake here — the name reads like a\ntranslation model you could call per request. It is a real-time conversation model built on\nGemini 3 Pro. For translating recorded audio in a batch pipeline, use a normal Flash tier with a\ntranslation instruction and a `responseSchema`.\n\n**Every TTS model is preview.** There is no GA text-to-speech id. Anything shipping TTS carries\npreview risk — say so rather than implying stability.\n\n### Other modalities\n\n- **Image**: `gemini-3.1-flash-image` (Nano Banana 2) · `gemini-3.1-flash-lite-image` ·\n  `gemini-3-pro-image` (Nano Banana Pro, 4K) · `gemini-2.5-flash-image`\n- **Embeddings**: `gemini-embedding-001` (text) · `gemini-embedding-2-preview` (multimodal —\n  text, images, video, audio, PDFs)\n- **Video**: `gemini-omni-1.1-flash` (GA) · `veo-3.1-generate-preview` ·\n  `veo-3.1-lite-generate-preview`\n- **Music**: `lyria-3.5` · `lyria-3-clip-preview` · `lyria-3-pro-preview` · `lyria-realtime-exp`\n- **Agents (preview)**: `gemini-2.5-computer-use-preview-10-2025` ·\n  `deep-research-preview-04-2026` · `deep-research-max-preview-04-2026` ·\n  `antigravity-preview-05-2026` · `gemini-robotics-er-2-preview` ·\n  `gemini-robotics-er-1.6-preview`\n\n### Never use — shut down or deprecated\n\n`gemini-2.0-flash` · `gemini-2.0-flash-lite` · `gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview` ·\n`gemini-3-pro-preview` · `imagen-4.0-generate`\n\n### SDK\n\n- **`@google/genai` v2.0.0+**. Pin v2+; the Interactions API broke at v2. Latest 2.21.0.\n- `ai.models.generateContent({ model, contents, config })`\n- Construct the client server-side only. An API key in browser-reachable code is a leaked key.\n- Flash supports Search grounding, Maps grounding, File Search, Code Execution, URL Context,\n  and standard function calling.\n\n## Tier selection\n\nTwo capability levers: **model** and **thinking level**. Pull both deliberately.\n\n- Smarter (harder reasoning, more reliable tool use) → higher tier and/or higher thinking.\n- **Split a multi-call pipeline by job.** Narrow boolean classification belongs on Flash-Lite;\n  the one call doing real extraction or transformation belongs on `gemini-3.8-flash`. Running\n  every call at the top tier buys latency and cost, not accuracy.\n- **Less** optimal (sub-optimal persona, \"play like a beginner\", looser output) → Flash-Lite\n  and/or `MINIMAL` thinking. A bigger model optimizes *harder*; reaching for Pro to make\n  something play dumber moves the wrong way. The prompt (negative constraints + few-shot) is\n  the primary lever; the smaller tier reinforces it.\n\n## Prompting Flash\n\nFlash 3.5 favors directness over verbosity, logic over persuasion. The prompt is a control\nsurface, not a conversation. Chain-of-thought prose in the prompt hurts — move reasoning to\nthe thinking level.\n\n**Structure**\n\n- XML tags **or** markdown headers, never both — mixing muddies the instruction/data boundary.\n- Order: `role → instructions → constraints → output`.\n- Role + behavioral constraints go in `systemInstruction`. It persists across turns, so each\n  user turn carries only the immediate task.\n- Data first, task/question **last**. Bridge with \"Based on the information above, …\".\n\n**Negative constraints** — state them explicitly, placed next to the instruction they guard,\nnot in a far-off rules dump. Plain and direct (\"Do NOT reach for the most-efficient question\")\nbeats hedging.\n\n**Few-shot** — 2–3 examples are the strongest lever for style, persona, and format; stronger\nthan describing the behavior in prose. Match example complexity to the real task. For a\npersona, *show* one turn of it.\n\n**Thinking level** — set `thinkingConfig: { thinkingLevel }`, never \"think step by step\" in\nthe prompt.\n\n- `MINIMAL` — fast; also stops a capable model from over-reasoning into behavior you did not\n  want (optimal play when you asked for casual).\n- `MEDIUM` / `HIGH` — genuine multi-step reasoning where accuracy beats speed.\n\nReplacing verbose CoT prompting with a raised thinking level is a strict improvement. Delete\nthe CoT.\n\n**Temperature** — 1.0 default. Lower only for deterministic extraction/classification.\n\n**Structured output** — anything beyond trivial JSON gets a `responseSchema` (types, `enum`,\n`propertyOrdering`) plus `responseMimeType: 'application/json'`. The schema enforces shape, so\nstop babysitting it in prose. Still re-validate the parsed object in code: schema-valid and\nsemantically wrong is reachable, and a model response is untrusted input.\n\n## Template\n\n```\n<role>You are [persona] doing [task]. [One line on how it reasons.]</role>\n\n<how_you_work>\n- [Imperative steps, one trait/action each.]\n</how_you_work>\n\n<never>\n- [Explicit negative constraints, each next to the behavior it guards.]\n</never>\n\n<examples>\n- [1–3 short worked examples that show the desired behavior/persona/format.]\n</examples>\n\n<output>[What to return; pair with a responseSchema for JSON.]</output>\n```\n\n`config`: `systemInstruction` = the above · `responseSchema` for JSON · `thinkingLevel` per\ntask · `temperature: 1` unless deterministic. Data goes in `contents`, task last.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Models — <https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models>\n- What's new in Gemini 3.5 Flash — <https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/whats-new-gemini-3.5>\n- Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite — <https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-3.1-flash-lite>\n- Prompt design strategies — <https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/prompting-strategies>\n- Gemini 3 prompting (philschmid) — <https://www.philschmid.de/gemini-3-prompt-practices>\n- Gemini 3 prompting guide (Google Cloud) — <https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/start/gemini-3-prompting-guide>\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: artifact-capabilities\nPath: bundled:artifact-capabilities\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities\n\n# Artifact runtime capabilities\n\nA published Artifact page can declare **runtime capabilities** — abilities the claude.ai viewer grants the page at open time — by passing `capabilities: {name: config}` to the Artifact tool. The control plane is the authority on valid names and config shapes. Declaration gestures: **omitting** `capabilities` on a redeploy carries the stored declaration forward unchanged (and preserves the artifact's stored contract pin); an **empty object** `{}` is the explicit clear-all; a **non-empty object** is a full-set declaration (anything stored but not restated is revoked). Moving a republished artifact's runtime version is a deliberate gesture — pass `contract: 'latest'` to upgrade, or a specific version to pin or roll back — never a side effect of editing.\n\n**A page that republishes itself** through the `artifact` capability sends its whole document in exactly the shape the Artifact tool publishes, so a later publish from the tool recognizes and replaces the skeleton instead of nesting it: `<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset=utf8><meta name=viewport content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover\"><style>` the same small reset the tool's description names `</style></head><body>` — no whitespace between those tags and nothing else in the head — then the page content exactly as it was written for the tool (its `<title>` and `<style>` first, inside the body, regenerated from the page's state), then `</body></html>`.\n\n**Available capabilities:** `artifact`, `assets`, `comments`, `db`, `downloads`, `files`, `mcp`, `room`, `sample`, `self`, `user` — the complete set of capability names you may declare; built in on every page, called without declaring (never pass these in `capabilities`): `permissions`. Anything not listed is unavailable to this user.\n\n> Tool spelling in this session: the `Artifact` tool's `action: \"read_db\"` / `\"write_db\"` with a `db_op` are the `ArtifactData` tool, whose `action` is that `db_op` (\"get\", \"list\", \"query\", \"set\", \"update\", \"str_replace\", \"delete\", \"batch\") with the other fields unchanged — load it with ToolSearch when you first need it. Read the steps below with that substitution.\n\nRuntime contract 0.2.91\n\n\nCapability namespaces live behind `claude.use(name)`: `const db = await claude.use(\"db\")` resolves the capability's namespace, or `null` when this view cannot run it (not served, not granted, or failed to load — indistinguishable by design). Branch on `null` and design for absence. `window.claude` carries only `use`: no `window.claude.db`, `.room`, or `.artifact` member is ever promised, so never read one — render the page without them and light features up when the promise resolves (later, never within your script's first run, and unordered with DOMContentLoaded; `null` after 10 s when no viewer answers). The resolved namespace is frozen and platform-owned: call its functions and keep the reference; never assign to it, `defineProperty` on it, or replace a member (wrap it for your own helpers). Permission stays on the calls: a consent prompt, rate limit, or policy refusal arrives on the first call, never from `use()`. Awaiting `use(\"db\")` again is free (memoized); an unknown name resolves `null`.\n\n\n--- capability: artifact ---\n\nUse `artifact` for pages that should remember what people do with them: polls, sign-up sheets, checklists, trackers, boards — the page is the record; data kept server-side, or seeded or read back by Claude, is `db`. Declare `capabilities: {artifact: {}}`; `const artifact = await claude.use(\"artifact\")`, then `await artifact.publish(html)` saves `html` (a complete document, doctype first) as the new version, and every open view, this one included, reloads to it. Nothing a viewer types, ticks or drags is kept unless the page publishes it. So embed the shared state as data in the HTML you publish and render the page from it; when an interaction completes, update the state, regenerate the document and publish it — never serialize the live DOM; batch rapid edits into one publish; publish only after a viewer acts, never on load. `conflict` is routine (every view reloads to the winner, dropping this edit): no retry. For read-only viewers publish rejects `not_granted`/`not_writer` — render a read-only view.\n\n\n--- capability: assets ---\n\n`assets` stores uploaded assets: `const assets = await claude.use(\"assets\")`; `assets.upload(blob)` (image, SVG, video, PDF, font, CSS/JS, or CSV/Markdown/JSON/text; 20 MiB cap, CSS/JS 16 MiB, SVG 2 MiB and sanitized) resolves `{id, url, sizeBytes, contentType}`; `assets.list()` resolves `{assets, usage}`; `assets.delete(id)` removes one for good: only on a deliberate user action, updating rows that held it. Declare `capabilities: {assets: {}}`: only Editors and up get it, others get `null` from `use(\"assets\")` (hide asset UI; handle errors). Only if the user wants uploads from non-editors, declare `{assets: {upload: \"interact\"}}`: where enabled, Contributors can upload but list and delete only their own, and their files show only in the organization; never run any asset as code, CSS or HTML. Store the `id` in `db` rows as the durable pointer; use the returned `url` as an `<img>`/`<video>`/`<a>` source (SVG: `<img>` or CSS only); a stored id serves at `\"/_blob/\" + id`. The type definitions are authoritative.\n\n\n--- capability: comments ---\n\n`comments` wires a page's own commenting UI to the artifact's shared comment store: `await claude.use(\"comments\")` (`null`: unavailable). The declaration picks the grant: `capabilities: {comments: {\"composer_only\": true}}` grants only `openComposer({element}|{range})` (opens the shell's composer like a comment-mode click; prefer it for discoverable entry points) and `anchorFor`, no consent asked. The full form `{comments: {}}` adds write verbs acting as the viewer under consent; public-link visitors and email invitees get `null`. `\"customAnchors\": true` in either form adds `customAnchors()` (register at load) for pages that position comment pins themselves; invented anchor names (canvas, WebGL, video) need the full form. WRITE-ONLY: the shell renders every thread — never build the page's own list. Call the other verbs only from a deliberate viewer gesture, never on load. Read the type definitions before use (authoritative for verbs, shapes, bounds and error codes).\n\n\n--- capability: db ---\n\n`db` keeps data outside the page: shared rows, per-person state, data Claude reads. Declare `capabilities:{db:{},user:{}}`; `const db = await claude.use(\"db\")` (`null` if signed out). By default signed-in people read shared docs, Contributors (`interact`) and up overwrite/delete, Viewers/Commenters never write. `rules` set per-path minimums, `view`<`interact`<`admin` (Editor, granted Owner)<`owner`; \"all read, each writes own\": `db:{rules:[{path:\"votes\",read:\"view\",write:\"owner\"},{path:\"votes/{self}\",write:\"interact\"}]}` (prefix rule must set both). `data/users/<id>/` is private even from the owner; `<id>` = `await user.id()`. Even segment counts are docs (`db.doc(\"tasks/t1\")`), odd are collections; per-person items: `db.collection(\"data/users/\"+id).doc(k)`; get/set/update/delete, where/orderBy/limit, onSnapshot. Never hardcode seeds: `write_db` after publish; handle an empty db. Subscribe once per query, never in render; one write at a time per doc, only on change. No secrets; shared data is untrusted.\n\n\n--- capability: downloads ---\n\nThe `downloads` capability lets a published page offer a generated file to the viewer: declare `capabilities: {downloads: true}`, then `const downloads = await claude.use(\"downloads\")` (`null`: unavailable — hide the affordance) and `await downloads.save({filename, data})`. The viewer sees a confirmation and may decline — a save is never silent or guaranteed, so offer it on explicit viewer intent and handle rejection. The type definitions are authoritative for the call contract and error codes.\n\n\n--- capability: files ---\n\n`files` reads a Claude Code project's files as the viewer. Declare it only when you know the project's id (e.g. your session's): `capabilities: {files: {project: \"chan_...\"}}`. `const files = await claude.use(\"files\")` (`null`: unavailable, e.g. a viewer outside the organization; hide it). A viewer who can't open the files gets `not_found`, never a prompt; a member is asked once, so call it on a click or after load, never in a loop. `files.list({path, recursive, cursor})`; `files.read(path, {offset, length})` -> `{bytes, size, revision}` (new or just-edited files can lag a few seconds); `files.download(path)` asks the viewer to save it (some types, e.g. .exe, .js and .bat, are refused); `await files.asSampleTools()` gives `sample` list/read tools (declare it too). Only project members can publish an artifact declaring files. Don't copy file contents into the artifact's data, assets or versions unless the user asks: anyone it's shared with can read them. The type definitions give shapes and error codes.\n\n\n--- capability: mcp ---\n\n`mcp` lets a page call the viewer's claude.ai connectors: `await claude.use(\"mcp\")` (`null`: unavailable); calls use the viewer's credentials, never exposing tokens. Declare `capabilities: {mcp: {servers: [{server, tools}]}}`; `server` is a connector's display name, or `host:<name>` for a local MCP server on the viewer's device (Claude app only; else `server_not_connected`). Keep the manifest minimal: a viewer-consented grant; outside viewers get `null`. Two arms: DISPLAYING data registers `watchTool(server, tool, input, handler, opts?)` (replays cache, refreshes when stale, polls only via `refetchInterval`); an ACTION calls `callTool` once and reads `result.payload` or `(await server(name)).<tool>(input)` for the payload. Tool failures REJECT (`tool_error`); watches get error events. Branch UX per error code, retry only `retryable` errors, drop data on authz denials, show freshness (`cache.storedAt`). Types omit tool arguments: read `describeTool` or observe a real call, else say so at publish; never guess.\n\n\n--- capability: permissions ---\n\n`permissions` is built in — call it, never declare it in `capabilities`. Prompts are lazy by default: a capability that needs consent asks at its first use — never block first paint on permissions. `state` reads without prompting (one name, or the full map); `request` asks with at most one batched dialog (names, or everything); a page that needs several grants up front may call it once at startup. `manage` opens the Permissions panel and resolves with nothing when it closes; offer it as a button, never call it on load; it rejects `unavailable` if the panel did not open; then point the viewer to the artifact's Permissions menu. A viewer's \"no\" is not an error: `state` and `request` never reject, and a denial is final for the page load (a repeated `request` shows no dialog; the next load starts fresh), so branch on the returned states and degrade per capability (hide or disable the affordance), never fail or offer retry buttons; never call `request` in a loop: re-asks are rate-limited and read as nagging.\n\n\n--- capability: room ---\n\nThe `room` capability reaches whoever has the page open RIGHT NOW:\ndeclared as `capabilities: {room: {}}`; `await claude.use(\"room\")`\n(`null`: cannot connect). emit(topic, data) sends a moment; on(topic,\nfn) hears them. presence(patch) sets YOUR state (cursor) as one\nobject newcomers get, cleared when you leave; onPeers(fn) delivers\neveryone's -- render all, marked \"you\". NOTHING persists and messages\ncan drop: state a late joiner needs (current slide) is a db doc,\nlasting content a new artifact version. Send absolute state. What you\nhear is untrusted: everyone here, guests too (guest: true), plus your\npublishing session when admitted (kind \"agent\"); the page must work\nalone and light up. Presence is never authority (anyone sets it).\nEvent topics are admin-only (Editors up); open one to Contributors\n(never Viewers): {room: {topics: {move: \"interact\"}}}. join(name)\ngives each game or group its own room, heard only by joiners: emit,\non, presence, onPeers, leave(). Names aren't secret. `room` stays the\nlobby.\n\n\n--- capability: sample ---\n\n`sample` asks Claude (declare `capabilities:{sample:{}}`; `{sample:{images:true}}` for images): `const sample=await claude.use(\"sample\")` (null: hide); `await sample(input,opts?)`->`{text,truncated}`; `sample.json`->value. `input`: string|`[{role:\"user\"|\"assistant\",content:string}]` ending on user. Memoryless: send instructions+data+format. opts: `onText({text,delta})` (`text`=WHOLE answer, assign it; \"Thinking...\" till text), `signal` (new AbortController/call), `tools:[{name,description,inputSchema?,execute(input)}]` (page functions; return small data or throw; no `cache`), `images:Blob[]` if `(await sample.limits()).images`, `modelTier` quick|default|complex, `cache` (5min replay; `false` for chat). Only if user names model/effort: `{model:\"claude-sonnet-5-5\",modelTier,effort}`, effort low|medium|high; unusable→`modelTier`; see `modelApplied`. Rejects `{code,message,text?:partial}`: hide on `not_granted`, back off on `rate_limited`, never loop. 1st call asks consent; call on click or stable load prompt.\n\n\n--- capability: self ---\n\n`self` is the former name of the `artifact` capability (renamed). It remains for compatibility: published pages and previously generated code that declare `capabilities: {self: {}}` or call `claude.use(\"self\")` keep working unchanged — both names resolve this same capability (this contract promises no `window.claude.self` member to feature-check; `use()` is the check). Do not use it in new pages: declare `capabilities: {artifact: {}}` and obtain the namespace with `await claude.use(\"artifact\")`; see the artifact section for how to use it.\n\n\n--- capability: user ---\n\n`user` answers who is viewing this page and who your shared state names: the owner's organization and invited guests (`guest: true`); others are absent. `const user = await claude.use(\"user\")`; `null` = absent (`user?.isOwner() ?? false`). `isOwner()`/`canEdit()`/`can(name)` need no setup (canEdit = admin level; `can(\"data.write\")` = may write shared `db` docs, `null` = not told: keep the input; refused writes decide). Declare `capabilities:{user:{}}` for `id()`/`me()` (opaque id, comparable only within one scope; `me()` never null) and `profiles(ids)`; `scopes:[\"profile\"]` adds names and `search(q)`; `[\"profile\",\"email\"]` adds addresses. Reads never reject. Store only ids (`id()` or `hit.id`), never a name, avatar, or Profile: names differ per viewer and go stale. Resolve in render, every time: `const ps = await user.profiles(idsOnScreen)` then `ps[id].name || 'Someone'` (cached: call again freely). `name` is `\"\"` if unresolvable: use `||` not `??`. Call `search('')` on focus; set names with textContent.\n\n\n**Your connectors this session.** Connector tools appear in your tool list as `mcp__<connector>__<toolName>`. Set `server` to the `<connector>` segment — everything between `mcp__` and the next `__` (for `mcp__claude_ai_Slack_beta__search`, the `server` is `claude_ai_Slack_beta`). Copy the segment exactly, case included; when publishing, it is resolved to the connector's display name automatically. In the page's own `callTool`/`watchTool` calls, pass the connector's display name (its name as shown in claude.ai), not that segment — viewers resolve connectors by name only. The publish result states the exact display name for each segment it resolves; if the page's calls do not match it, fix them and publish again. Locally-configured MCP servers connected in this session can also be declared, as host servers: set `server` to `host:<server>` where `<server>` is the segment between `mcp__` and the next `__` in that server's tool names (`mcp__filesystem__read_file` → `host:filesystem`). Only servers from the user's MCP configuration count, with one built-in exception: `host:claude_browser` is the Claude app's own browser — declare it, with the tools the page needs from `read_page`, `get_page_text`, `find`, `preview_start`, `navigate`, `computer` and `form_input`, when the page must read or act on other websites; it answers only when the viewer opens the page in the Claude desktop app; the viewer is asked once, at the page's first call to it, and again at each click or key press that calls a banking or other high-risk site. The app's other built-in servers (`cowork`, `scheduled-tasks`, `session_info`, `workspace` and the like) are never host servers, and a page that declares one is refused at publish. A host server only answers when the viewer opens the page in a Claude app that has that same local server connected — say so to the user when you publish. The manifest's `tools` array takes the connector's upstream tool names (as returned by `listTools()` / `/v1/mcp_servers`), which can differ from the normalized `<toolName>` segment when an upstream name contains `.` or spaces. Every `servers[]` entry needs a non-empty `tools` array naming the tools the page calls — an empty or omitted `tools` list is refused and never means \"all tools\"; to publish without connector access, leave `mcp` out of `capabilities` (pass `capabilities: {}` to clear a stored declaration) rather than declaring an empty `servers` list. In hermetic/CI sessions where connectors aren't loaded but `$CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` is set, fetch the list via Bash: `curl -H 'anthropic-version: 2023-06-01' -H 'anthropic-beta: mcp-servers-2025-12-04' -H \"Authorization: Bearer $CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\" https://api.anthropic.com/v1/mcp_servers?limit=1000`; in that case use each entry's `display_name` as the `server` value (exact display names are always accepted alongside tool-prefix segments).\n\n**Call contract** (runtime contract 0.2.91). The platform-served `window.claude` type definitions for this contract are extracted under `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities`: `0.2.91/artifact.d.ts`, `0.2.91/assets.d.ts`, `0.2.91/claude.d.ts`, `0.2.91/comments.d.ts`, `0.2.91/db.d.ts`, `0.2.91/downloads.d.ts`, `0.2.91/files.d.ts`, `0.2.91/mcp.d.ts`, `0.2.91/permissions.d.ts`, `0.2.91/room.d.ts`, `0.2.91/sample.d.ts`, `0.2.91/self.d.ts`, `0.2.91/user.d.ts`. Read `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities/0.2.91/claude.d.ts` (how a page reaches any capability on this contract) and `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.311/f4c003b475a5cc42c5e9c60098f16cc5/artifact-capabilities/0.2.91/mcp.d.ts` before writing any code that calls the `mcp` capability — they are authoritative for this contract version over any remembered API shape. Open these files with the Read tool rather than `cat`: a file past the Bash tool's inline output limit does not come back in full. The type definitions cover only the call envelope, not a connector tool's argument names or result shape. Take argument names from the tool's input schema in this session's own definition of that connector tool, when it is loaded here. Learn a result's shape from one real call of a tool that is safe to run — never run a write only to learn its result. The published page may also read a connector tool's schema itself with `describeTool(server, tool)` at view time, once the viewer has allowed that connector for the page (viewers without that support reject it — treat any rejection as no schema available); this session cannot read that answer before publishing, so it is no substitute for a schema read here. If this session has no schema for a tool and cannot safely call it, say so to the user at publish time — in your reply, not as a note inside the published page — instead of shipping a guessed shape. Observed response payloads are the user's real data: learn \n\n[... skill content truncated for compaction; use Read on the skill path if you need the full text]\n\n---\n\n### Skill: generate-commit-message\nPath: userSettings:generate-commit-message\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/anchildress1/.claude/skills/generate-commit-message\n\nProduce exactly one Conventional Commits v1.0.0 message from the diff, then commit it signed.\nThe commit is the deliverable — no draft file, no push.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nThe only repo-mutating commands permitted are `git commit -S` and path-explicit `git add` under\n[Staging](#staging). Everything else is off-limits:\n\n| Never | Why |\n| - | - |\n| Add to a non-empty index | A staged diff is the user's scope decision; commit exactly it |\n| `git add -A` / `.` / `-u` / globs | Sweeps unrelated edits into the commit |\n| `git push`, `rebase`, `reset`, `commit --amend` | History mutation isn't this skill's job |\n| `--no-verify`, `--no-gpg-sign` | Hooks and signing must run |\n| Edit source, docs, or config | No formatters, lint fixes, refactors, or test runs |\n| Commit on `main`/`master` | Unless the user directed it **this turn** |\n| Omit the AI-attribution trailer | A message without it is a failed message |\n\n## Abort conditions\n\nStop and report; do not work around:\n\n- No staged diff and the request names no work to commit (\"commit\" alone, tree holds several\n  changes) → `nothing staged`; ask which change.\n- On `main`/`master` without explicit authorization this turn.\n- Diff changed between derivation and commit (see step 5).\n\n## Staging\n\nOnly when the index is empty.\n\n- Stage by explicit path every file — tracked and untracked — that belongs to the change the user\n  named this turn (\"commit the fiji work\", \"commit this\").\n- Dirty paths outside that change, or a file mixing it with another concern → stop, list them, ask.\n  A pre-commit hook that re-stages fixed files can sweep unstaged edits into the commit.\n- Report the staged paths with the SHA.\n\n## Splitting one working tree into atomic commits\n\nOnly on an explicit request this turn.\n\nRun steps 1–7 once per group, deriving each message from that group's staged diff alone.\n\n- One logical change per commit. A subject that needs \"and\" is two commits.\n- Every commit stands alone: the tree builds and imports resolve at **each** SHA, not just at the\n  tip. Verify before moving to the next group.\n- Never hand-split hunks in a file carrying two concerns — that is where work silently disappears.\n  Instead: copy the finished files outside the repo, revert the *other* concern out of the file and\n  commit, restore the copy and commit the remainder, then diff the tree against the copy. A\n  non-empty diff means the split dropped work.\n- Report every SHA and subject, not just the last.\n\n## 1. Resolve scope\n\nPrecedence, first match wins. Jira key regex `[A-Z][A-Z0-9]+-\\d+`, matched case-insensitively,\nemitted uppercase:\n\n1. User-provided key (request or `argument-hint`)\n2. Branch name — `git --no-pager rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`\n3. None — omit the scope\n\nNever invent a keyword scope (`core`, `api`, `docs`) when no issue key exists. An absent scope is\ncorrect; a made-up one is noise. The diff informs type and subject, never scope.\n\n## 2. Acquire diff\n\n```bash\ngit --no-pager diff --cached\n```\n\nEmpty → run [Staging](#staging), then re-run this command. Never derive from `git diff` of the\nworking tree: `git commit` records the index, so unstaged edits are not in the commit.\n\nDerive everything from hunks only: chat history, prior commits, and memory are not evidence of\nwhat this diff changes.\n\n## 3. Pick type\n\nFirst match wins:\n\n| Signal | Type |\n| - | - |\n| Public API removed, signature changed, config key dropped | breaking — add `!` and/or `BREAKING CHANGE:` |\n| New user-visible capability | `feat` |\n| Defect repair, no new surface | `fix` |\n| Faster, same behavior | `perf` |\n| Restructure, same behavior | `refactor` |\n| Whitespace, formatting, semicolons | `style` |\n| Tests only | `test` |\n| Docs, comments, JSDoc/KDoc only | `docs` |\n| `.github/workflows`, `.circleci`, CI config | `ci` |\n| Build system, deps, packaging | `build` |\n| Reverts a prior commit | `revert` |\n| Maintenance, no production impact | `chore` |\n\n## 4. Pick attribution tier\n\nExactly one, from diff hunks + this session:\n\n| AI share of the work | Trailer |\n| - | - |\n| Majority | `Generated-by` |\n| Roughly half | `Co-authored-by` |\n| Minor assist | `Assisted-by` |\n| Only wrote this message | `Commit-generated-by` |\n\nUncertain → pick the tier that credits the human more.\n\n`Co-authored-by` is the one tier GitHub actually parses and renders on the commit, which is the\npoint of it — don't substitute a variant spelling. The other three are custom trailers that git\ncarries verbatim.\n\n## 5. Compose and validate\n\n```\n<type>[(<scope>)][!]: <subject>\n\n- <single-line bullet>\n- <single-line bullet>\n\n<footers>\n```\n\nFormat constraints — every one maps to a commitlint rule, so a violation is a hook failure, not a\nstyle opinion:\n\n- Subject ≤ 72 chars, no trailing period, imperative present tense. Not Title Case, sentence-case,\n  PascalCase, or UPPER — but real identifiers keep their casing (`OAuth2`, `JSDoc`, `API`)\n- Type lowercase, from the enum in step 3\n- Body and footer lines ≤ 100 chars; bullets never wrap\n- Exactly one blank line after the subject, exactly one before the footers, none elsewhere\n- Group changes and say *what* and *why*. File-by-file listings and *how* are wasted lines.\n  Quantify where it helps (\"add 3 fixtures\", \"drop 412 LOC\").\n\nFooter order, when present:\n\n1. `Refs: PROJ-123` / `Closes #42`\n2. `BREAKING CHANGE: <incompatibility and required action>` — only when the `!` alone doesn't\n   tell the user what to do. Both forms together are valid.\n3. AI attribution — required, exactly one, from step 4.\n\nTrailers use `-` for spaces (`Reviewed-by`); `BREAKING CHANGE` is the spec's one exemption. A\nfooter too long to fit in 100 chars folds onto a continuation line with a leading space.\n\n**Match the repo before emitting.** Read `commitlint.config.js` (or `.commitlintrc*`) if present\nand honor overrides — `scope-case`, stricter lengths, a narrowed type enum. Then check\n`git --no-pager log -3` for trailers this repo actually uses (`Signed-off-by`, `Refs`) and mirror\nthem. A message that passes the generic spec but trips this repo's hook is still a failure.\n\nThen verify before committing:\n\n- Header matches `^(build|chore|ci|docs|feat|fix|perf|refactor|revert|style|test)(\\([^)]+\\))?!?: .+$`\n- No line over its limit; exactly one attribution trailer present\n\nFailing any check → regenerate. Never emit a non-conformant message.\n\n## 6. Re-check the diff\n\nRe-run the step 2 command immediately before committing. If it changed, the message describes\nwork that no longer matches — re-derive from step 2. If the diff can't be re-read, say the\nmessage may be stale and proceed.\n\n## 7. Commit\n\n```bash\ngit commit -S -F - <<'EOF'\n<full message including blank lines and footers>\nEOF\n```\n\n`-F -` preserves blank lines and trailers exactly; `-m` with embedded newlines is where formatting\ngets mangled. Report the short SHA and subject.\n\nOn failure — hook rejection, denied permission, signing error — do not retry blindly and do not\namend. Print the composed message in a fenced block so the work isn't lost, surface the exact\nfailure output, and state what the user likely needs to do. Then stop; the underlying problem is\ntheirs to resolve, not something to work around.\n\n## Examples\n\n```\nfix: pin qs to 6.14.2 to address prototype-pollution vulnerability\n\n- add npm override for qs across the dependency tree\n- align instantsearch.js resolution to the pinned version\n\nAssisted-by: Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n```\n\n```\nfeat(PROJ-123): add OAuth2 device-code flow to login\n\n- support polling the token endpoint with backoff\n- surface user_code and verification_uri in CLI output\n- cover happy-path and timeout in unit tests\n\nRefs: PROJ-123\nGenerated-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n```\n\n```\nfeat(PROJ-456)!: drop deprecated /v1 endpoints\n\n- remove /v1/users and /v1/sessions handlers\n- migrate fixture suite to /v2 equivalents\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: clients pinned to /v1 must upgrade to /v2 before this release.\nGenerated-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n```\n\n\nThe following tools just became available and are ready to use:\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__batch\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__guide\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__update\n\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nArtifactComments\nArtifactData\nCronCreate\nCronDelete\nCronList\nDesignSync\nEndConversation\nEnterPlanMode\nEnterWorktree\nExitPlanMode\nExitWorktree\nLSP\nListMcpResourcesTool\nMonitor\nNotebookEdit\nPushNotification\nReadMcpResourceDirTool\nReadMcpResourceTool\nRemoteTrigger\nSendMessage\nTaskStop\nWebFetch\nWebSearch\nmcp__Neon__add_auth_oauth_provider\nmcp__Neon__add_auth_trusted_domain\nmcp__Neon__compare_database_schema\nmcp__Neon__complete_database_migration\nmcp__Neon__complete_query_tuning\nmcp__Neon__create_auth_user\nmcp__Neon__create_branch\nmcp__Neon__create_credential\nmcp__Neon__create_postgres_database\nmcp__Neon__create_postgres_endpoint\nmcp__Neon__create_postgres_role\nmcp__Neon__cr